window.CONCEPTS = window.CONCEPTS || {};
(function(){
const R = String.raw;

/* ===================== EXAM 1 ===================== */

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/exam1/ct"] = [
  R`<b>Setup.</b> A pencil beam enters a material at $x=0$; $Y_x$ is the (random) number of photons still travelling at depth $x$ and $\lambda_x=E[Y_x]$ its expectation. $\mu(x)$ is the linear attenuation coefficient in cm$^{-1}$, $x$ in cm, so $\mu\,dx$ is dimensionless (Tomography notes).`,
  R`<b>Beer–Lambert ODE.</b> The absorption rate is proportional to the number of photons and the density: $\dfrac{d\lambda_x}{dx}=-\mu(x)\lambda_x$. Separate variables and integrate: $\lambda_x=\lambda_0\exp\!\Big\{-\int_0^x\mu(t)\,dt\Big\}$. Always show the two lines of derivation; that is where the points are.`,
  R`<b>The projection integral.</b> Taking the log converts multiplicative physics into an additive line integral: $\displaystyle\int_0^T\mu(t)\,dt=-\log\frac{\lambda_T}{\lambda_0}$. This is the quantity tomography needs, one number per ray.`,
  R`<b>Poisson counting.</b> $P\{Y_x=k\}=\dfrac{e^{-\lambda_x}\lambda_x^k}{k!}$, $E[Y_x]=\mathrm{Var}(Y_x)=\lambda_x$. The measured count $Y_T$ is a noisy version of $\lambda_T$, so the estimate is $\hat y=-\log(Y_T/\lambda_0)$ (or $-\log(Y_T/Y_0)$ with a measured blank).`,
  R`<b>Blank scan vs. object scan.</b> The blank (air) scan measures the dosage $\lambda_0$ together with source non-uniformity, detector gain and exposure time; the object scan measures $\lambda_T$ with the same factors. Their ratio cancels every unknown gain, leaving $e^{-\int\mu}$. Transmission $=Y_T/Y_0\in(0,1]$; sinogram value $=-\log(\text{transmission})$.`,
  R`<b>Piecewise-constant slabs.</b> For $\mu=\mu_0$ on a length $L$, $\lambda_T=\lambda_0e^{-\mu_0L}$ and $y=\mu_0L$. Sketches: $y$ is a straight line in $\mu_0$ (or in $L$) through the origin; $\lambda_T$ is a decaying exponential starting at $\lambda_0$, reaching $\lambda_0/e$ at $L=1/\mu_0$. Segments add: $y=\sum_i\mu_iL_i$.`,
  R`<b>Photon starvation.</b> Because $\lambda_T$ falls exponentially with attenuation, thick or dense paths (bone, metal) receive few photons; when $Y_T=0$ the estimate $-\log 0=\infty$ is undefined. Remedies: clamp counts, raise dose, or use a Poisson-likelihood model-based reconstruction.`,
  R`<b>Noise in the log estimate.</b> Delta method: $\hat y=-\log Y_T+\log\lambda_0$, $d\hat y/dY_T=-1/Y_T$, so $\mathrm{Var}(\hat y)\approx\lambda_T/\lambda_T^2=1/\lambda_T=e^{y}/\lambda_0$. Projection noise grows exponentially with the attenuation along the ray; the noisiest rays create streaks after back-projection.`,
  R`<b>Energy and wavelength.</b> Photon energy $E=h\nu=hc/\lambda_{\rm w}$; higher keV means shorter wavelength. For most soft materials $\mu$ decreases with energy (e.g. polyethylene about 0.21 cm$^{-1}$ at 40 keV vs 0.17 cm$^{-1}$ at 80 keV), so more photons get through at high energy. A polychromatic source therefore gives a nonlinear (concave) $y(L)$: beam hardening (final-exam topic).`,
  R`<b>Where the ray comes from.</b> In the Radon geometry the ray at angle $\theta$ and offset $r$ has $y=p_\theta(r)=\int f\,dz$ with $f=\mu$; the pin-hole collimator selects one line. CT collects $p_\theta(r)$ for all $(\theta,r)$ and inverts with convolution back-projection (see the Radon topic).`,
  R`<b>Emission tomography contrast.</b> In PET the object emits: $E[y_i]=\sum_jA_{ij}x_j$ with $x_j$ the tracer emission rate and $A_{ij}$ the detection probability; coincident gamma rays define the line. Still Poisson counts, but linear in the unknown rather than exponential (Tomography notes).`,
  R`<b>Units checklist.</b> $\mu$ [cm$^{-1}$], $x,L,T$ [cm], $\lambda,Y$ [photons, dimensionless counts], $y=\int\mu\,dx$ [dimensionless]. Label axes on every sketch: exams grade the units.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/exam1/plane"] = [
  R`<b>CSFT definition.</b> $F(u,v)=\displaystyle\iint f(x,y)e^{-j2\pi(ux+vy)}dxdy$, $f(x,y)=\iint F(u,v)e^{j2\pi(ux+vy)}dudv$. $u$ is horizontal frequency (vertical stripes), $v$ vertical frequency (horizontal stripes); $y$ conventionally points down (raster order). Units of $u,v$: cycles per unit length (CSFT notes).`,
  R`<b>Standard functions.</b> $\mathrm{rect}(t)=1$ for $|t|\le\tfrac12$; $\mathrm{sinc}(t)=\dfrac{\sin\pi t}{\pi t}$; $\Lambda(t)=(1-|t|)_+$; $\mathrm{rect}(x,y)=\mathrm{rect}(x)\mathrm{rect}(y)$, $\mathrm{sinc}(x,y)=\mathrm{sinc}(x)\mathrm{sinc}(y)$, $\mathrm{circ}(x,y)=\mathrm{rect}(\sqrt{x^2+y^2})$. $\delta(t)$ is defined by $\int\delta(t)g(t)dt=g(0)$, intuitively $\lim_{\epsilon\to0}\tfrac1\epsilon\mathrm{rect}(t/\epsilon)$.`,
  R`<b>1-D pairs to memorize.</b> $\delta(t)\leftrightarrow1$, $1\leftrightarrow\delta(f)$, $\mathrm{rect}(t)\leftrightarrow\mathrm{sinc}(f)$, $\mathrm{sinc}(t)\leftrightarrow\mathrm{rect}(f)$, $\Lambda(t)\leftrightarrow\mathrm{sinc}^2(f)$; with scaling $f(at)\leftrightarrow\tfrac1{|a|}F(f/a)$, e.g. $\mathrm{rect}(t/a)\leftrightarrow|a|\,\mathrm{sinc}(af)$ (CTFT notes).`,
  R`<b>Separability.</b> If $f(x,y)=g(x)h(y)$ then $F(u,v)=G(u)H(v)$: the 2-D CSFT is a 1-D CTFT in $x$ followed by a 1-D CTFT in $y$. Hence $\mathrm{rect}(x,y)\leftrightarrow\mathrm{sinc}(u,v)$, $\delta(x,y)\leftrightarrow1$, $\mathrm{sinc}(ax)\mathrm{sinc}(by)\leftrightarrow\tfrac1{|ab|}\mathrm{rect}(u/a)\mathrm{rect}(v/b)$, and $e^{-\pi(x^2+y^2)}\leftrightarrow e^{-\pi(u^2+v^2)}$. $\mathrm{circ}$ is not separable; its transform is $\mathrm{jinc}(u,v)=J_1(\pi\sqrt{u^2+v^2})/(2\sqrt{u^2+v^2})$, circularly symmetric.`,
  R`<b>A function of one variable only.</b> $f(x,y)=\delta(x)$ (a line along the $y$ axis) equals $\delta(x)\cdot1$, so $F(u,v)=1\cdot\delta(v)$: a line along the $u$ axis. Likewise $\mathrm{sinc}(ax)\leftrightarrow\tfrac1a\mathrm{rect}(u/a)\delta(v)$. A line in space is a perpendicular line in frequency.`,
  R`<b>Property table.</b> Linearity; conjugation $f^*\leftrightarrow F^*(-u,-v)$; scaling $f(ax,by)\leftrightarrow\tfrac1{|ab|}F(u/a,v/b)$; shift $f(x-x_0,y-y_0)\leftrightarrow e^{-j2\pi(ux_0+vy_0)}F$; modulation $e^{j2\pi(u_0x+v_0y)}f\leftrightarrow F(u-u_0,v-v_0)$; convolution $\leftrightarrow$ product; product $\leftrightarrow$ convolution; duality $F(x,y)\leftrightarrow f(-u,-v)$; inner product $\iint fg^*=\iint FG^*$ (Parseval).`,
  R`<b>Plane wave pair.</b> $\delta(u-u_0,v-v_0)$ inverts to $e^{j2\pi(u_0x+v_0y)}$, so $$\cos\big(2\pi(u_0x+v_0y)\big)\leftrightarrow\tfrac12\big[\delta(u-u_0,v-v_0)+\delta(u+u_0,v+v_0)\big].$$ For a sine use $\sin\theta=\tfrac1{2j}(e^{j\theta}-e^{-j\theta})$: the two impulses have weights $\pm\tfrac1{2j}$.`,
  R`<b>Geometry of the wave.</b> Spatial frequency magnitude $f_0=1/P_0=\sqrt{u_0^2+v_0^2}$; peak-to-peak spacing $P_0=1/f_0$; propagation direction $\phi=\arctan(v_0/u_0)$ (the direction of the vector $(u_0,v_0)$). Crests are straight lines perpendicular to $(u_0,v_0)$. Rotating the image rotates the spectrum by the same angle.`,
  R`<b>Slices through the wave.</b> Along $y=0$ the signal is $\cos(2\pi u_0x)$ with frequency $u_0\le f_0$ and period $1/u_0=P_0/\cos\phi$ (stretched); along the propagation direction (unit vector $d$, arc length $r$) it is $\cos(2\pi f_0r)$. Sketch both with $P_0$ and $\phi$ labelled.`,
  R`<b>Rotation property.</b> For an orthonormal rotation $A$ ($|A|=1$, $A^{-1}=A^t$): $g(A[x,y]^t)\leftrightarrow G(A[u,v]^t)$. General linear map: $f(A\mathbf x)\leftrightarrow\tfrac1{|\det A|}F(A^{-t}\mathbf u)$. Example: $\mathrm{rect}\big(\tfrac{y+x}{\sqrt2},\tfrac{y-x}{\sqrt2}\big)\leftrightarrow\mathrm{sinc}\big(\tfrac{v+u}{\sqrt2},\tfrac{v-u}{\sqrt2}\big)$ (CSFT notes).`,
  R`<b>Modulated sinc.</b> $\mathrm{sinc}(x,y)\cos(2\pi(u_0x+v_0y))\leftrightarrow\tfrac12\mathrm{rect}(u-u_0,v-v_0)+\tfrac12\mathrm{rect}(u+u_0,v+v_0)$: two unit boxes at $\pm(u_0,v_0)$; centre frequency $f_c=\sqrt{u_0^2+v_0^2}$, bandwidth 1 per axis. Highest frequency along an axis is $\max(|u_0|,|v_0|)+\tfrac12$.`,
  R`<b>Convolving sincs.</b> $\mathrm{sinc}(at)*\mathrm{sinc}(bt)\leftrightarrow\tfrac1{ab}\mathrm{rect}(f/a)\mathrm{rect}(f/b)=\tfrac1{ab}\mathrm{rect}\big(f/\min(a,b)\big)$, so the result is $\tfrac1{\max(a,b)}\mathrm{sinc}(\min(a,b)t)$: the narrower band wins. Conversely $\mathrm{rect}*\mathrm{rect}=\Lambda$.`,
  R`<b>Nyquist for a plane wave.</b> Rectangular sampling with period $T$ is alias-free iff $\tfrac1{2T}>\max(|u_0|,|v_0|)$, i.e. $T&lt;\dfrac1{2\max(|u_0|,|v_0|)}$; for the modulated sinc add the half-bandwidth: $T&lt;\dfrac1{2(\max(|u_0|,|v_0|)+\frac12)}$. Under-sampling folds $(2\pi u_0T,2\pi v_0T)$ back into $[-\pi,\pi]^2$ and changes both frequency and orientation (Sampling notes).`,
  R`<b>Sketching spectra.</b> Draw impulses as arrows with their weights, boxes with their heights ($1/|ab|$ for scaled rects) and widths; mark the $u$ and $v$ axes in cycles/mm. Real even inputs give real even spectra; a shift adds only a linear phase, so $|F|$ is unchanged.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/exam1/lsi"] = [
  R`<b>2-D FIR filter.</b> $y(m,n)=\sum_{k=-N}^{N}\sum_{l=-N}^{N}h(k,l)\,x(m-k,n-l)$: $(2N+1)^2$ multiplies per output pixel. $H(z_1,z_2)=\sum h(k,l)z_1^{-k}z_2^{-l}$ and the DSFT $H(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})=\sum h(k,l)e^{-j(k\mu+l\nu)}$ is the $z$-transform on the unit "sphere" $z_1=e^{j\mu},z_2=e^{j\nu}$ (Filters, DTrans notes).`,
  R`<b>DSFT basics.</b> $X(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})=\sum_m\sum_nx(m,n)e^{-j(\mu m+\nu n)}$, inverse $x=\tfrac1{4\pi^2}\iint_{-\pi}^{\pi}Xe^{j(\mu m+\nu n)}d\mu d\nu$; $2\pi$-periodic in both $\mu$ and $\nu$, so $\mathrm{rect}(\mu)$ alone is not a valid DSFT. Convolution $\leftrightarrow$ product, shift $\leftrightarrow e^{-j(\mu m_0+\nu n_0)}$, product $\leftrightarrow\tfrac1{(2\pi)^2}$ periodic convolution. No scaling or rotation properties exist for the DSFT.`,
  R`<b>DC gain.</b> $H(e^{j0},e^{j0})=\sum_{k,l}h(k,l)$. Image filters are normally normalized to DC gain 1 so mean brightness is preserved; difference filters ($\delta-h$ with $\sum h=1$, derivative kernels) have DC gain 0. Maximum response of a symmetric low-pass complement is at $(\mu,\nu)=(\pi,\pi)$.`,
  R`<b>Separability.</b> $h(m,n)=h_1(m)h_2(n)\Rightarrow H(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})=H_1(e^{j\mu})H_2(e^{j\nu})$, and 2-D convolution becomes 1-D row filtering followed by 1-D column filtering: $p+q$ multiplies per pixel instead of $pq$. A psf is separable iff its matrix has rank 1 (every row a multiple of one row). To prove non-separability, exhibit two rows whose entry ratios differ.`,
  R`<b>Worked kernels.</b> $h_1=\tfrac14[1,2,1]\leftrightarrow\tfrac14(e^{j\omega}+2+e^{-j\omega})=\tfrac12(1+\cos\omega)$, so the $3\times3$ binomial $\tfrac1{16}[1\,2\,1]^t[1\,2\,1]$ has $H=\tfrac14(1+\cos\mu)(1+\cos\nu)$: low-pass with $H(0,0)=1$, zero at $(\pi,\pi)$. $h_1=\tfrac14[1,-2,1]\leftrightarrow-\tfrac12(1-\cos\omega)$ gives the high-pass $\tfrac14(1-\cos\mu)(1-\cos\nu)$ with $H(0,0)=0$. Also $\tfrac13[1,1,1]\leftrightarrow\tfrac13(1+2\cos\omega)$, $[1,-1]\leftrightarrow1-e^{-j\omega}$ (Filters notes Examples 1–2).`,
  R`<b>Unsharp mask / sharpening.</b> $y=x+\lambda(x-h*x)$ has psf $p=(1+\lambda)\delta-\lambda h$ and $P(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})=1+\lambda\big(1-H(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})\big)$. With $\sum h=1$: $P(0,0)=1$ (brightness kept); where $H=0$, $P=1+\lambda$ is the maximum boost. $\lambda>0$ emphasizes high frequencies (sharpens, may ring); $\lambda&lt;0$ attenuates them (blurs). $p$ is generally not separable even when $h$ is, because a rank-1 matrix plus a multiple of $\delta$ has rank up to 2.`,
  R`<b>Sketching $P$.</b> Along $\nu=0$ with the binomial $h$ and $\lambda=1$: $P=\tfrac32-\tfrac12\cos\mu$, from 1 at DC to 2 at $\mu=\pm\pi$. Draw the $2\pi$-periodic curve and label the values at $0$ and $\pi$.`,
  R`<b>Boundary conditions.</b> Notes use the free boundary (pixels outside the image are 0); alternatives are toroidal (wrap) and reflective. Work through the example: a $16$-valued region filtered by the binomial kernel yields $1,3,4$ on the corner/edge ramps (Filters notes p. 2).`,
  R`<b>Derivative kernels.</b> Horizontal derivative $\tfrac1{16}\begin{bmatrix}2&0&-2\\4&0&-4\\2&0&-2\end{bmatrix}$ is separable: $\tfrac14[1,2,1]^t$ smoothing across rows times $\tfrac12[1,0,-1]$ differencing; the vertical one is its transpose. Output is signed, zero on flat regions.`,
  R`<b>2-D IIR filters and ordering.</b> $y_s=\sum_rb_rx_{s-r}+\sum_{r>0}a_ry_{s-r}$ needs an ordering of $\mathbb Z^2$: quarter-plane, symmetric half-plane, or non-symmetric half-plane (NSHP: $s&lt;r$ iff $s_2&lt;r_2$ or $s_2=r_2,s_1&lt;r_1$, i.e. raster order). Multiplies per pixel $=(2N+1)^2+2(P+1)P$. Recursion must use only already-computed outputs; the "symmetric" recursion $y=x+a(y_{m\pm1}+y_{n\pm1})$ is not computable (Filters notes Example 5).`,
  R`<b>Transfer function of a recursion.</b> Take $z$-transforms: $Y(1-\sum a_rz^{-r})=X\sum b_rz^{-r}$, so $H=\dfrac{\sum b_rz_1^{-r_1}z_2^{-r_2}}{1-\sum a_rz_1^{-r_1}z_2^{-r_2}}$. Example 3: $y=x+ay(m-1,n)+ay(m,n-1)\Rightarrow H=\dfrac1{1-az_1^{-1}-az_2^{-1}}$, a low-pass whose impulse response spreads over the quarter plane (run the $a=\tfrac12$ example: $64,32,16,\dots$).`,
  R`<b>Separable IIR.</b> $y=ay(m-1,n)+by(m,n-1)-ab\,y(m-1,n-1)+x\Rightarrow H=\dfrac1{(1-az_1^{-1})(1-bz_2^{-1})}$, $h=a^mb^nu(m)u(n)$, stable iff $|a|&lt;1,|b|&lt;1$, DC gain $\tfrac1{(1-a)(1-b)}$. Implement as two 1-D recursions (2 multiplies instead of 3). Example 4 with a $+2a\,y(m+1,n-1)$ term is NSHP and gives a diagonally oriented response.`,
  R`<b>Stability and linearity checks.</b> FIR filters are always stable; an IIR filter is stable iff $h$ is absolutely summable (poles of each 1-D factor inside the unit circle for separable cases; in general 2-D there is no simple root test). Verify linearity ($T[\alpha x_1+\beta x_2]=\alpha y_1+\beta y_2$) and shift invariance before applying any transform reasoning.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/exam1/sampling"] = [
  R`<b>Rep and comb.</b> $\mathrm{rep}_T[x](t)=\sum_kx(t-kT)$ (periodic replication) and $\mathrm{comb}_T[x](t)=x(t)\sum_k\delta(t-kT)$ (impulse sampling). Transform pairs: $\mathrm{comb}_T[x]\leftrightarrow\tfrac1T\mathrm{rep}_{1/T}[X]$ and $\mathrm{rep}_T[x]\leftrightarrow\tfrac1T\mathrm{comb}_{1/T}[X]$; in 2-D $\mathrm{comb}_{X,Y}[f]\leftrightarrow\tfrac1{XY}\mathrm{rep}_{1/X,1/Y}[F]$ (RepComb notes). Sampling in space is replication in frequency.`,
  R`<b>1-D sampling theorem.</b> $s(n)=g(nT)\Rightarrow S(e^{j\omega})=\dfrac1T\sum_kG\Big(\dfrac{\omega-2\pi k}{2\pi T}\Big)$. Recipe: rescale $f\to\omega=2\pi fT$ (so $f=\tfrac1{2T}\mapsto\pi$, $f=f_s\mapsto2\pi$), replicate with period $2\pi$, multiply by $1/T$ (Sampling notes p. 1–2).`,
  R`<b>2-D sampling theorem.</b> $s(m,n)=g(mT_x,nT_y)\Rightarrow S(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})=\dfrac1{T_xT_y}\sum_k\sum_lG\Big(\dfrac{\mu-2\pi k}{2\pi T_x},\dfrac{\nu-2\pi l}{2\pi T_y}\Big)$; $(u,v)=(\tfrac1{2T_x},\tfrac1{2T_y})\mapsto(\pi,\pi)$.`,
  R`<b>Nyquist condition.</b> $g$ is uniquely recoverable from $s$ if $G(u,v)=0$ for $|u|>\tfrac1{2T_x}$ or $|v|>\tfrac1{2T_y}$; sufficient, not necessary (a non-rectangular spectral support can tile without overlap at a lower rate, Example 3). Reconstruction: $g(x,y)=\sum_{m,n}s(m,n)\,\mathrm{sinc}\big(\tfrac{x-mT}{T}\big)\mathrm{sinc}\big(\tfrac{y-nT}{T}\big)$, equivalently $G(u,v)=T^2\,\mathrm{rect}(Tu,Tv)\,S(e^{j2\pi Tu},e^{j2\pi Tv})$.`,
  R`<b>Sampled sinc worked example.</b> $g(t)=\mathrm{sinc}(t/\tau)\leftrightarrow\tau\,\mathrm{rect}(\tau f)$, band limit $\tfrac1{2\tau}$, Nyquist $T&lt;\tau$. For $T&lt;\tau$: boxes of height $\tau/T$ and width $2\pi T/\tau$ centred on multiples of $2\pi$, with gaps. At $T=\tau$: $s(n)=\mathrm{sinc}(n)=\delta(n)$, $S\equiv1$, boxes tile exactly. For $T>\tau$ boxes overlap (aliasing), and e.g. $\mathrm{sinc}(2n)$ is still $\delta(n)$: the samples cannot tell $\mathrm{sinc}(2t)$ from $\mathrm{sinc}(t)$.`,
  R`<b>Reading aliased spectra.</b> Fold any frequency outside $[-\pi,\pi]$ back by multiples of $2\pi$; a plane wave at $(2\pi u_0T,2\pi v_0T)$ with $|2\pi u_0T|>\pi$ appears at a lower frequency and possibly a different orientation. Overlapping replicas add, so a flat spectrum can result from severe aliasing.`,
  R`<b>Focal-plane array (CMOS/CCD) model.</b> Each cell integrates photons over a $T\times T$ square: $s(m,n)=\iint h(x-mT,y-nT)g(x,y)\,dxdy$ with $h=\tfrac1{T^2}\mathrm{rect}(x/T,y/T)$. Equivalently $\tilde g=h(-x,-y)*g$ followed by ideal sampling $s=\tilde g(mT,nT)$: the aperture blurs, then samples. Cells should be large for sensitivity, which violates the ideal sampling assumption.`,
  R`<b>FPA in frequency.</b> $\tilde G(u,v)=H^*(u,v)G(u,v)=\mathrm{sinc}(Tu,Tv)G(u,v)$, then $$S(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})=\frac1{T^2}\sum_{k,l}\mathrm{sinc}\Big(\frac{\mu-2\pi k}{2\pi},\frac{\nu-2\pi l}{2\pi}\Big)G\Big(\frac{\mu-2\pi k}{2\pi T},\frac{\nu-2\pi l}{2\pi T}\Big).$$ The sinc is $0.637$ at the band edge $\mu=\pi$ and acts as a mild anti-alias filter. For a fill factor $a$ (cell side $aT$) the sinc becomes $\mathrm{sinc}(aTu,aTv)$: less blur, less light.`,
  R`<b>Display / reconstruction model.</b> A monitor renders $f(x,y)=\sum_{m,n}s(m,n)\,p(x-mT,y-nT)$ with pixel psf $p$; in frequency $F(u,v)=P(u,v)\,S(e^{j2\pi Tu},e^{j2\pi Tv})$ (substitute $\mu\to2\pi Tu$). Square pixels $p=\mathrm{rect}(x/T,y/T)$ give $P=T^2\mathrm{sinc}(Tu,Tv)$; the display softens the image further.`,
  R`<b>Complete chain.</b> $F(u,v)=\dfrac{P(u,v)}{T^2}\sum_{k,l}H^*\!\big(u-\tfrac kT,v-\tfrac lT\big)G\big(u-\tfrac kT,v-\tfrac lT\big)$; with no aliasing only $k=l=0$ survives: $F=\tfrac{P(u,v)H^*(u,v)}{T^2}G=\mathrm{sinc}^2(Tu,Tv)\,G(u,v)$ for square detectors and square pixels. Gain $0.637^2=0.405$ at the band edge; scanned images normally need sharpening (Sampling notes p. 14–15).`,
  R`<b>Compensation filter.</b> Insert a digital filter $H_c(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})=1/\mathrm{sinc}^2\big(\tfrac\mu{2\pi},\tfrac\nu{2\pi}\big)$ on $[-\pi,\pi]^2$ so that $F=G$ in band; its gain is $1/0.405=2.47$ at the edge, so noise is amplified and the boost is usually limited. With fill factor $a$ the required edge gain drops (1.74 for $a=\tfrac12$).`,
  R`<b>Non-rectangular sampling.</b> If $f(\mathbf x)=\tilde f(A\mathbf x)$ then $F(\mathbf u)=\tfrac1{|\det A|}\tilde F(A^{-t}\mathbf u)$; sampling $f$ on the square grid $T(m,n)$ samples $\tilde f$ on the lattice $TA(m,n)^t$ (rotated/scaled). A rotation by $\theta$ with scale $c$ turns a disk of radius $\rho_c$ into radius $c\rho_c$, so $T&lt;\tfrac1{2c\rho_c}$.`,
  R`<b>Rate conversion links.</b> Decimation by $D$: pre-filter with $H=\mathrm{prect}_{2\pi/D}$, $h=\tfrac1D\mathrm{sinc}(n/D)$, then $y(n)=x(Dn)$ with $Y=\tfrac1D\sum_{k=0}^{D-1}X(e^{j(\omega-2\pi k)/D})$. Upsampling by $L$: zero-insert ($Y=X(e^{j\omega L})$) then $H=L\,\mathrm{prect}_{2\pi/L}$, $h=\mathrm{sinc}(n/L)$; pixel replication and bilinear ($\Lambda(m/L)\Lambda(n/L)$) are cheap approximations (RateConversion notes).`,
  R`<b>Raster order and vectorization.</b> Pixels are ordered left to right, top to bottom; $x=\mathrm{vec}(f)$ with $f(m,n)=x_{nM+m}$. An image is data (a vector), not a matrix (a linear operator); matrix notation transposes rows and columns ($f_{n,m}=f(m,n)$).`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/exam1/radon"] = [
  R`<b>Rotated coordinates.</b> $A_\theta=\begin{bmatrix}\cos\theta&-\sin\theta\\\sin\theta&\cos\theta\end{bmatrix}$ (counter-clockwise), $[x,y]^t=A_\theta[r,z]^t$, inverse $[r,z]^t=A_{-\theta}[x,y]^t$, so $r=x\cos\theta+y\sin\theta$ and $z=-x\sin\theta+y\cos\theta$. $|A_\theta|=1$, $A_\theta^{-1}=A_\theta^t$.`,
  R`<b>Radon transform / forward projection.</b> $p_\theta(r)=\displaystyle\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f\big(A_\theta[r,z]^t\big)dz=\int f(r\cos\theta-z\sin\theta,\;r\sin\theta+z\cos\theta)\,dz$: integrate along the line at signed distance $r$ from the origin, perpendicular to the direction $\theta$. The $r=0$ line passes through the origin; $p_\theta(r)$ for all $\theta\in[0,\pi)$ and $r$ is the sinogram (Tomography notes).`,
  R`<b>Mass conservation.</b> $\int p_\theta(r)\,dr=\iint f\,dxdy=F(0,0)$ for every $\theta$: use it to check any computed projection (a unit square gives area 1 at every angle).`,
  R`<b>Fourier slice theorem.</b> With $P_\theta(\rho)=\mathrm{CTFT}\{p_\theta(r)\}$: $P_\theta(\rho)=F(\rho\cos\theta,\rho\sin\theta)$. The 1-D transform of a projection is the 2-D CSFT evaluated on the radial line at angle $\theta$; $P_\theta(\rho)$ is $F$ in polar coordinates.`,
  R`<b>Proof sketch.</b> $P_\theta(\rho)=\iint f(A_\theta[r,z]^t)e^{-j2\pi\rho r}\,dz\,dr$; change variables $[r,z]^t=A_{-\theta}[x,y]^t$ (Jacobian 1) and use $r=x\cos\theta+y\sin\theta$ to get $\iint f(x,y)e^{-j2\pi(x\rho\cos\theta+y\rho\sin\theta)}dxdy=F(\rho\cos\theta,\rho\sin\theta)$. Alternative: prove for $\theta=0$ ($p_0(r)=\int f(r,y)dy\Rightarrow P_0(\rho)=F(\rho,0)$) and invoke the rotation property of the CSFT.`,
  R`<b>Band-limit corollary.</b> If $F(u,v)=0$ for $\sqrt{u^2+v^2}>f_c$ then $P_\theta(\rho)=0$ for $|\rho|>f_c$: every projection of a band-limited image is band-limited to the same $f_c$, so detectors with pitch $T_d\le1/(2f_c)$ sample projections without aliasing.`,
  R`<b>Shift property.</b> $f(x-x_0,y-y_0)\mapsto p_\theta\big(r-x_0\cos\theta-y_0\sin\theta\big)$: the $z$-component of the shift disappears in the integral. A point $\delta(x-x_0,y-y_0)$ therefore traces the sinusoid $r=\sqrt{x_0^2+y_0^2}\cos(\theta-\phi_0)$ in the sinogram, which is why it is called a sinogram.`,
  R`<b>Standard projections.</b> Unit disk $\mathrm{circ}$: $p_\theta(r)=2\sqrt{1-r^2}\,\mathrm{rect}(r/2)$ for all $\theta$ (chord length). Gaussian $e^{-(x^2+y^2)/2}$: $\sqrt{2\pi}e^{-r^2/2}$. Unit square $\mathrm{rect}(x,y)$: $\mathrm{rect}(r)$ at $\theta=0,\pi/2$ and, via $F=\mathrm{sinc}(u)\mathrm{sinc}(v)$, $P_{\pi/4}=\mathrm{sinc}^2(\rho/\sqrt2)\Rightarrow p_{\pi/4}(r)=\sqrt2\,\Lambda(\sqrt2 r)$. Delta at origin: $\delta(r)$.`,
  R`<b>Linearity and scaling.</b> Projection is linear, so projections of sums add; $f(x/a,y/a)$ has $p_\theta(r)\to a\,p_\theta(r/a)$. Rotating the image by $\theta_0$ shifts the sinogram in $\theta$.`,
  R`<b>Inverse via polar inverse CSFT.</b> $f=\iint F e^{j2\pi(xu+yv)}dudv$ with $du\,dv=|\rho|\,d\rho\,d\theta$ becomes $f(x,y)=\int_0^\pi\Big[\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}|\rho|P_\theta(\rho)e^{j2\pi\rho(x\cos\theta+y\sin\theta)}d\rho\Big]d\theta$. Direct polar-to-Cartesian interpolation of $F$ is avoided by convolution back-projection.`,
  R`<b>Convolution back-projection (CBP/FBP).</b> (1) measure $p_\theta(r)$; (2) filter each projection $g_\theta(r)=h(r)*p_\theta(r)$ with $H(\rho)=|\rho|$ (the ramp); (3) back-project $f(x,y)=\int_0^\pi g_\theta(x\cos\theta+y\sin\theta)\,d\theta\approx\tfrac\pi M\sum_{m=0}^{M-1}g_{m\pi/M}(x\cos\theta_m+y\sin\theta_m)$. Back-projection smears each filtered projection along its lines and sums over angles.`,
  R`<b>Band-limited ramp filter.</b> Real filters cut off at $f_c$: $H(\rho)=f_c\big[\mathrm{rect}(\rho/2f_c)-\Lambda(\rho/f_c)\big]$, $h(r)=f_c^2\big[2\,\mathrm{sinc}(2f_cr)-\mathrm{sinc}^2(f_cr)\big]$. With detector pitch $T_d$ and $f_c=\tfrac1{2T_d}$ the discrete filter is $h(n)=\tfrac1{T_d}\big[\tfrac14\delta(n)-\tfrac14\mathrm{sinc}^2(n/2)\big]$: $\tfrac1{4T_d}$ at $n=0$, $0$ for even $n\ne0$, $-\tfrac1{T_d(\pi n)^2}$ for odd $n$.`,
  R`<b>Why filter at all.</b> Back-projecting unfiltered projections gives $f*\tfrac1{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}$ because the CSFT of $1/r$ is $1/\rho$ (the missing $|\rho|$ Jacobian); the ramp undoes this blur. Deconvolving $1/\rho$ is benign since it has no zeros (final-exam topic).`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/exam1/mri"] = [
  R`<b>Larmor precession.</b> A nucleus in field magnitude $M$ precesses at $\omega_0=LM$ rad/s ($L$ the Larmor constant, written $\gamma$ in exam problems). Hydrogen: $42.58$ MHz/T, so $63.87$ MHz at 1.5 T and $127.74$ MHz at 3 T; carbon-13: $10.705$ MHz/T. No ionizing radiation; expensive superconducting magnet (MRI notes).`,
  R`<b>Gradients.</b> $M(x,y,z,t)=M_0+xG_x(t)+yG_y(t)+zG_z(t)$: small, time-varying linear gradients make the resonance frequency depend on position, $\omega(x,t)=L\big(M_0+G(t)x\big)=\omega_0+LG(t)x$.`,
  R`<b>Slice selection.</b> Turn on $G_z>0$ only; resonance $\omega=L(M_0+zG_z)$. An RF pulse $s(t)=e^{j2\pi f_ct}\mathrm{sinc}(t\Delta f)$ has $S(f)=\mathrm{rect}\big((f-f_c)/\Delta f\big)$ and excites the slice centred at $z_c$ with thickness $\Delta z$ when $f_c=f_0+\tfrac{z_cLG_z}{2\pi}$ and $\Delta f=\tfrac{\Delta zLG_z}{2\pi}$.`,
  R`<b>Phase accumulation.</b> Frequency is the derivative of phase: $\tfrac{d\phi}{dt}=LM(x,t)$, so $\phi(x,t)=\int_0^tLM(x,\tau)d\tau=\omega_0t+x\,k(t)$ with $k(t)=L\int_0^tG(\tau)d\tau$ [rad/cm]; in 2-D $\phi=\omega_0t+xk_x(t)+yk_y(t)$. Assume $\phi(0)=0$ at excitation.`,
  R`<b>Signal from one voxel / element.</b> $r(x,t)=a(x)e^{j\phi(x,t)}dx$ where $a(x)$ (or $f(x,y)$) is the tissue-dependent weighting (proton density, $T_1$, $T_2$, $T_2^*$ weighted). The received signal is the sum over the rod or slice.`,
  R`<b>Received signal is a Fourier sample.</b> $r(t)=\displaystyle\int a(x)e^{j(\omega_0t+xk(t))}dx=e^{j\omega_0t}\int a(x)e^{jxk(t)}dx=e^{j\omega_0t}A\big(-k(t)\big)$ with $A(k)=\int a(x)e^{-jkx}dx$ (rad/cm), i.e. $e^{j\omega_0t}A_f\big(-\tfrac{k(t)}{2\pi}\big)$ in cycles/cm. In 2-D: $R(t)=e^{j\omega_0t}F\big(-\tfrac{k_x(t)}{2\pi},-\tfrac{k_y(t)}{2\pi}\big)$ where $F$ is the CSFT of $f(x,y)$.`,
  R`<b>k-space interpretation.</b> After demodulation $R(t)e^{-j\omega_0t}$ reads the CSFT of the object at the point $(k_x(t),k_y(t))$. $G_x(t),G_y(t)$ set the velocity through k-space ($\dot k=LG$); the gradient-coil voltages set the acceleration ($k\propto\iint v$). Scan a set of frequencies, then reconstruct with an inverse Fourier transform.`,
  R`<b>Readout with a constant gradient.</b> $G(t)=G_0$ gives $k(t)=LG_0t+k_{\min}$: a straight line through k-space. A negative pre-phasing lobe of duration $\tau$ sets $k_{\min}=-LG_0\tau$ so that both halves of k-space (negative and positive frequencies) are covered during $T_{\rm acq}=2\tau$.`,
  R`<b>Sampling in k-space.</b> Sampling every $\Delta t$ gives $\Delta k=LG_0\Delta t$; the reconstructed object is periodic with field of view $\mathrm{FOV}=2\pi/\Delta k$, which must exceed the object size ($\Delta t\le\tfrac{2\pi}{LG_0L_{\rm obj}}$) to avoid wrap-around aliasing. Resolution $\delta x\approx\pi/k_{\max}$: stronger or longer gradients give finer resolution.`,
  R`<b>Reconstruction recipe.</b> Demodulate, map sample $n$ to $A$ at $k=-(k_{\min}+n\Delta k)$ (reverse the order because of the minus sign), fftshift so $k=0$ is central, inverse FFT, fftshift again; the result is $a(x)$ on a grid of spacing $\mathrm{FOV}/N$. In 2-D arrange the samples on the Cartesian grid and take a 2-D inverse DFT.`,
  R`<b>2-D Cartesian acquisition.</b> For each row: a $G_y$ blip whose area sets $k_y$ (phase encoding), a negative $G_x$ lobe, then constant $G_x$ readout sampling $N_x$ points. $N_y$ repetitions fill the grid; $\mathrm{FOV}_y=2\pi/\Delta k_y$, $\delta y\approx\pi/k_{y,\max}$; scan time grows with $N_y$.`,
  R`<b>Echo-planar imaging.</b> A serpentine raster through k-space: alternating $\pm G_x$ readout lobes with small $G_y$ blips between lines, acquired after a single excitation; fast but sensitive to gradient imperfections (MRI notes p. 15–16).`,
  R`<b>Connections.</b> MRI is sampling theory in the frequency domain: $\Delta k$ plays the role of $1/\mathrm{FOV}$ (replication in space), $k_{\max}$ the role of the band limit (resolution), exactly dual to the Sampling notes.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/exam1/dsft"] = [
  R`<b>Definition and inverse.</b> $G(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})=\sum_m\sum_ng(m,n)e^{-j(\mu m+\nu n)}$, $g(m,n)=\tfrac1{4\pi^2}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\!\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}G\,e^{j(\mu m+\nu n)}d\mu\,d\nu$. Periodic with period $2\pi$ in each of $\mu,\nu$; $\mu$ pairs with the horizontal index $m$, $\nu$ with $n$ (DTrans notes).`,
  R`<b>Row/column decomposition.</b> Define the row DTFT $R(m,\nu)=\sum_ng(m,n)e^{-j\nu n}$. Then $G(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})=\sum_mR(m,\nu)e^{-j\mu m}$: the DSFT is the DTFT along $m$ of the DTFT along $n$, in either order (Fubini for absolutely summable $g$). Same structure as the CSFT separability proof.`,
  R`<b>Projections.</b> Horizontal projection $p_h(m)=\sum_ng(m,n)$ and vertical $p_v(n)=\sum_mg(m,n)$. Since $R(m,0)=\sum_ng(m,n)=p_h(m)$, the DTFT of $p_h$ is $P_h(\mu)=G(e^{j\mu},e^{j0})$; likewise $P_v(\nu)=G(e^{j0},e^{j\nu})$. Discrete Fourier slice theorem: axis projections give axis slices of the spectrum.`,
  R`<b>Total mass.</b> $\sum_mp_h(m)=\sum_np_v(n)=\sum_{m,n}g(m,n)=G(e^{j0},e^{j0})$, the DC value. Both projections must therefore have the same sum; a useful consistency check.`,
  R`<b>Two projections do not determine the image.</b> Knowledge of $P_h,P_v$ fixes $G$ only on the two axes $\nu=0$ and $\mu=0$; off-axis values are free. Counterexample: $\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{bmatrix}$ and $\begin{bmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{bmatrix}$ (or any two $3\times3$ arrays with equal row and column sums). Continuous CT needs all angles $\theta$ for the same reason.`,
  R`<b>Diagonal and general projections.</b> Summing along the diagonal $m=n+c$ gives the slice $G(e^{j\mu},e^{-j\mu})$; in general summing along the direction $(a,b)$ gives the slice perpendicular to it. Discrete geometry limits the available angles.`,
  R`<b>Property table.</b> Linearity; conjugation $g^*\leftrightarrow G^*(e^{-j\mu},e^{-j\nu})$; shift $g(m-m_0,n-n_0)\leftrightarrow e^{-j(\mu m_0+\nu n_0)}G$; modulation $e^{j(\mu_0m+\nu_0n)}g\leftrightarrow G(e^{j(\mu-\mu_0)},e^{j(\nu-\nu_0)})$; convolution $\leftrightarrow$ product; product $\leftrightarrow\tfrac1{(2\pi)^2}$ periodic convolution; Parseval $\sum fg^*=\tfrac1{(2\pi)^2}\iint FG^*$. Separable $f(m)g(n)\leftrightarrow F(e^{j\mu})G(e^{j\nu})$. No scaling/rotation properties.`,
  R`<b>Useful DTFT pairs.</b> $\delta(n)\leftrightarrow1$; $1\leftrightarrow2\pi\sum_k\delta(\omega-2\pi k)$; $\mathrm{pulse}_N(n)=u(n)-u(n-N)\leftrightarrow\mathrm{psinc}_N(\omega)e^{-j\omega(N-1)/2}$ with $\mathrm{psinc}_N=\tfrac{\sin(\omega N/2)}{\sin(\omega/2)}$; $\mathrm{sinc}(Tn)\leftrightarrow\tfrac1T\mathrm{prect}_{2\pi T}(\omega)$; $\tfrac{\omega_0}\pi\mathrm{sinc}(\tfrac{\omega_0n}{\pi})\leftrightarrow\mathrm{prect}_{2\omega_0}(\omega)$, where $\mathrm{prect}_{2\omega_0}(\omega)=\sum_k\mathrm{rect}\big(\tfrac{\omega-2\pi k}{2\omega_0}\big)$.`,
  R`<b>Geometric sequence.</b> $a^mb^nu(m)u(n)\leftrightarrow\dfrac1{(1-ae^{-j\mu})(1-be^{-j\nu})}$ for $|a|,|b|&lt;1$. Its horizontal projection is $\tfrac{a^mu(m)}{1-b}$ with DTFT $\tfrac1{(1-ae^{-j\mu})(1-b)}=G(e^{j\mu},e^{j0})$, confirming the slice theorem.`,
  R`<b>Real and symmetric signals.</b> Real $g$ gives Hermitian $G$ ($G(e^{-j\mu},e^{-j\nu})=G^*$); real and even $g$ gives real even $G$, so $3\times3$ symmetric kernels have cosine-only transforms such as $\tfrac14(1+\cos\mu)(1+\cos\nu)$.`,
  R`<b>2-D z-transform.</b> $F(z_1,z_2)=\sum_{m,n}f(m,n)z_1^{-m}z_2^{-n}$; the DSFT is its evaluation at $z_1=e^{j\mu},z_2=e^{j\nu}$. Difference equations become rational $H(z_1,z_2)$, the route to IIR transfer functions (see LSI topic).`,
  R`<b>Frequency-domain interpretation of a sampled image.</b> Under Nyquist sampling, $G(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})=\tfrac1{T^2}G_c\big(\tfrac{\mu}{2\pi T},\tfrac{\nu}{2\pi T}\big)$ on $[-\pi,\pi]^2$, so DSFT slices correspond to CSFT slices and discrete projections approximate continuous ones.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/exam1/dtlti"] = [
  R`<b>DTFT and z-transform.</b> $X(e^{j\omega})=\sum_nx(n)e^{-j\omega n}$ ($2\pi$-periodic), $x(n)=\tfrac1{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}X(e^{j\omega})e^{j\omega n}d\omega$; $X(z)=\sum_nx(n)z^{-n}$ on its region of convergence (ROC), with $X(e^{j\omega})=X(z)|_{z=e^{j\omega}}$ when the unit circle is in the ROC (DTrans notes).`,
  R`<b>First-order recursion.</b> $y(n)=x(n)+ay(n-1)\Rightarrow Y(1-az^{-1})=X$, $H(z)=\dfrac1{1-az^{-1}}=\dfrac z{z-a}$, pole at $z=a$. Causal solution $h(n)=a^nu(n)$ with ROC $|z|>|a|$; the anti-causal solution $-a^nu(-n-1)$ has ROC $|z|&lt;|a|$: the difference equation alone does not fix $h$, the ROC does.`,
  R`<b>Stability.</b> BIBO stable iff $\sum|h(n)|&lt;\infty$ iff the ROC contains the unit circle. For a causal system this means all poles strictly inside the unit circle: $|a|&lt;1$. Then $|H(e^{j\omega})|=\dfrac1{\sqrt{1+a^2-2a\cos\omega}}$; $a>0$ is low-pass (peak $\tfrac1{1-a}$ at DC, $\tfrac1{1+a}$ at $\pi$), $a&lt;0$ high-pass.`,
  R`<b>Sketching magnitude responses.</b> Plot over $[-\pi,\pi]$, mark DC and $\pm\pi$ values, note the $2\pi$ periodicity; for $a=0.9$ the DC gain is 10 and the gain at $\pi$ is $0.526$. Use dB only if asked.`,
  R`<b>Cascade and parallel.</b> Series: $H=H_1H_2$, $h=h_1*h_2$ (order does not matter for LTI). Parallel: $H=H_1+H_2$. Two identical first-order sections: $H=\dfrac1{(1-az^{-1})^2}\leftrightarrow(n+1)a^nu(n)$. An FIR $1-az^{-1}$ cancels the pole and inverts the recursion.`,
  R`<b>FIR building blocks.</b> $[1,-1]$: $H=1-e^{-j\omega}=2je^{-j\omega/2}\sin(\omega/2)$, $|H|=2|\sin(\omega/2)|$, zero DC gain. $[1,1]$: $|H|=2|\cos(\omega/2)|$. $\tfrac14[1,2,1]$: $\tfrac12(1+\cos\omega)$. FIR systems are always stable; $z$-transforms are polynomials in $z^{-1}$ with ROC everything except $0$.`,
  R`<b>Eigenfunction property.</b> $x(n)=e^{j\omega n}\Rightarrow y(n)=H(e^{j\omega})e^{j\omega n}$; a cosine input gives $|H|\cos(\omega n+\angle H)$. This is why the frequency response fully describes an LTI system.`,
  R`<b>Linearity and time invariance.</b> Linear: $T[\alpha x_1+\beta x_2]=\alpha T[x_1]+\beta T[x_2]$. Time invariant: delaying the input delays the output. LTI $\Leftrightarrow$ convolution $y=h*x$. Non-examples: $y=x^2$ (TI, nonlinear), $y=nx(n)$ (linear, not TI), downsampling (linear, not TI), median (homogeneous, not additive).`,
  R`<b>Solving difference equations.</b> Take $z$-transforms (assume zero initial conditions), solve for $H(z)$, partial fractions, invert term by term using $a^nu(n)\leftrightarrow\tfrac1{1-az^{-1}}$. Or iterate the recursion for the first few samples and recognise the pattern.`,
  R`<b>Sampled-signal link.</b> If $x(n)=x_c(nT)$ then $X(e^{j\omega})=\tfrac1T\sum_kX_c\big(\tfrac{\omega-2\pi k}{2\pi T}\big)$ and $\omega=2\pi fT$; the ideal low-pass $\mathrm{prect}_{2\omega_c}$ has $h(n)=\tfrac{\omega_c}\pi\mathrm{sinc}(\tfrac{\omega_c}{\pi}n)$, and $\omega_c=\pi$ gives $\delta(n)$.`,
  R`<b>Periodicity pitfall.</b> A DTFT must satisfy $X(e^{j(\omega+2\pi)})=X(e^{j\omega})$; write ideal responses as periodic reps ($\mathrm{prect}$, $\mathrm{psinc}$) rather than a single rect, and sketch at least one full period beyond $\pm\pi$ when asked.`
];

/* ===================== EXAM 2 ===================== */

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/exam2/color"] = [
  R`<b>Color matching experiment.</b> A reference patch with spectrum $I(\lambda)$ is matched by an adjustable patch $r^+I_r(\lambda)+g^+I_g(\lambda)+b^+I_b(\lambda)$ built from three primaries $R,G,B$. Very saturated references cannot be matched with positive knobs, so a primary is added to the <em>reference</em> side instead; the net tristimulus value is $r=r^+-r^-$ and may be negative. Two spectra with equal tristimulus values are metamers (Tristimulus notes).`,
  R`<b>Grassmann's law.</b> Color perception is a 3-D linear space: if $I_1$ has $(r_1,g_1,b_1)$ and $I_2$ has $(r_2,g_2,b_2)$ then $I_1+I_2$ has the sum. Hence tristimulus values are linear functionals $r=\int_0^\infty r_0(\lambda)I(\lambda)d\lambda$ (same for $g,b$); the kernels $r_0,g_0,b_0$ are the color matching functions (CMFs).`,
  R`<b>Measuring CMFs.</b> Use a line spectrum $I(\lambda)=\delta(\lambda-\lambda_0)$ as reference: the matched tristimulus values are exactly $r_0(\lambda_0),g_0(\lambda_0),b_0(\lambda_0)$. Repeat over $\lambda_0$. CIE 1931 $2^\circ$ observer: primaries are spectral lines at 700, 546.1 and 435.8 nm; CMFs are normalised to unit area so equal-energy white $I(\lambda)=1$ gives $(r,g,b)=(1,1,1)$. The measured $r_0(\lambda)$ has a negative lobe.`,
  R`<b>CIE XYZ.</b> Defined as a linear combination $[x_0,y_0,z_0]^t=M[r_0,g_0,b_0]^t$ chosen so that all three CMFs are non-negative; then $[X,Y,Z]^t=M[r,g,b]^t$ for any spectrum. $Y$ is luminance, $X$ is red-ish, $Z$ blue-ish; always use capitals. Discretised: 31 samples 400–700 nm at 10 nm, $[X,Y,Z]^t=M_{xyz}I$ with the CMFs as rows.`,
  R`<b>XYZ primaries are imaginary.</b> Since $[X,Y,Z]=[R,G,B]M^{-1}$ and $M^{-1}$ has negative entries, the "spectra" of the XYZ primaries would have to be negative; no physical light realises them. They sit at chromaticities $(1,0)$, $(0,1)$, $(0,0)$, outside the horseshoe.`,
  R`<b>Chromaticity.</b> $x=\dfrac{X}{X+Y+Z}$, $y=\dfrac{Y}{X+Y+Z}$, $z=1-x-y$: hue and saturation without lightness; $(X,Y,Z)$ and $(\alpha X,\alpha Y,\alpha Z)$ share $(x,y)$. Projection property: straight lines in XYZ map to straight lines in $(x,y)$, because $C_3=\alpha C_1+\beta C_2$ gives $(x_3,y_3)=\alpha'(x_1,y_1)+\beta'(x_2,y_2)$ with $\alpha'=\alpha S_1/(\alpha S_1+\beta S_2)$, $S_i=X_i+Y_i+Z_i$ (ColorSpaces notes).`,
  R`<b>Chromaticity diagram.</b> The spectral locus (horseshoe) is the curve $\big(\tfrac{x_0(\lambda)}{x_0+y_0+z_0},\tfrac{y_0(\lambda)}{x_0+y_0+z_0}\big)$; the straight line of purples joins its 400 and 700 nm ends. Every real color is a positive mixture of spectral lines, hence lies in the convex hull. White points: equal energy $(\tfrac13,\tfrac13)$, D65 $(0.3127,0.3290)$ (6500 K, dominant standard), illuminant C $(0.310,0.316)$ (old NTSC).`,
  R`<b>Gamut triangle.</b> Additive mixtures of two colors lie on the segment between them, so three primaries span the triangle with vertices at their chromaticities; e.g. Rec. 709: $R(0.64,0.33)$, $G(0.30,0.60)$, $B(0.15,0.06)$. Colors outside the triangle need a negative tristimulus value and cannot be displayed. The region with $r&lt;0$ is the part of the horseshoe on the far side of the $G$–$B$ edge from $R$ (the line through $G$ and $B$ is where $r=0$).`,
  R`<b>Positive XYZ vs. real.</b> The XYZ triangle $(1,0),(0,1),(0,0)$ contains the whole horseshoe, so every real color has $X,Y,Z\ge0$. The converse fails: points inside the triangle but outside the horseshoe (e.g. near $(0.7,0.3)$ or $(0.05,0.05)$) have positive XYZ but correspond to no physical spectrum.`,
  R`<b>Building $M$ from chromaticities and a white point.</b> Column $k$ of $M$ is the XYZ of primary $k$, proportional to $[x_k,y_k,z_k]^t$, so $M=A\,\mathrm{diag}(\alpha_1,\alpha_2,\alpha_3)$ with $A=\begin{bmatrix}x_r&x_g&x_b\\y_r&y_g&y_b\\z_r&z_g&z_b\end{bmatrix}$. Requiring $(r,g,b)=(1,1,1)$ to produce the white point with $Y=1$: solve $A\alpha=\tfrac1{y_w}[x_w,y_w,z_w]^t$. For 709 primaries and D65 this yields $M=\begin{bmatrix}0.4124&0.3576&0.1805\\0.2126&0.7152&0.0722\\0.0193&0.1192&0.9505\end{bmatrix}$ (sRGB). For EE white the rows of $M$ simply sum to 1.`,
  R`<b>Reading $M$ and $M^{-1}$.</b> $[r,g,b]^t=M^{-1}[X,Y,Z]^t$; the rows of $M^{-1}$ applied to $[x_0,y_0,z_0]^t$ give the rgb CMFs of the display; $M\mathbf 1$ is the XYZ of white; the middle row of $M$ gives luminance $Y=0.2126r+0.7152g+0.0722b$. To classify a marked chromaticity point, form $[x,y,1-x-y]^t$, multiply by $M^{-1}$ and read off the signs of $(r,g,b)$.`,
  R`<b>Why display CMFs go negative but camera sensitivities cannot.</b> Any real primaries form a triangle strictly inside the horseshoe, so some spectral colors need a negative weight: the rgb CMFs $M^{-1}[x_0,y_0,z_0]^t$ must have negative lobes. A camera's spectral sensitivities count photons and are non-negative, so a camera cannot directly measure a display's rgb; it measures a positive basis (ideally a linear combination of XYZ) and a $3\times3$ matrix converts, exactly only if the sensitivities are linear combinations of the CMFs (Luther condition).`,
  R`<b>White point and gamma correction.</b> White point serves calibration (what $(1,1,1)$ means), color constancy and gamut mapping; $(x_w,y_w)$ does not determine $I_w(\lambda)$. Corrected data: $\tilde r=(r_{\rm lin}/r_{wp})^{1/\gamma}$ so $(\tilde r,\tilde g,\tilde b)=(1,1,1)$ reproduces the white point. sRGB: 709 primaries, D65, $\gamma\approx2.2$; convert with $r_{\rm lin}=(\tilde r/255)^{2.2}$ in floating point, then $M$; the reverse applies $M^{-1}$ then $255\,r_{\rm lin}^{1/2.2}$. Pitfall: apply $M$ only to linear values, and remember $(255,255,255)$ on an sRGB display is D65, not equal-energy white.`,
  R`<b>Subtractive color.</b> $(c,m,y)=(1-r,1-g,1-b)$ subtract from white; printing inks multiply reflectances, $R(\lambda)=R_C(\lambda)R_M(\lambda)I(\lambda)$, so inks interact nonlinearly (cyan plus magenta removes red and green, leaving blue). Additive mixing on displays is what Grassmann's law describes.`,
  R`<b>Beyond XYZ.</b> Lab is a nonlinear, approximately perceptually uniform space: $L=116f(Y/Y_0)-16$, $a=500[f(X/X_0)-f(Y/Y_0)]$, $b=200[f(Y/Y_0)-f(Z/Z_0)]$, $f\approx(\cdot)^{1/3}$; color error $\Delta E=\sqrt{\Delta L^2+\Delta a^2+\Delta b^2}$, valid for large uniform patches only. Opponent channels $O_1$ (luminance), $O_2=G-R$, $O_3=B-Y$ explain chroma subsampling (ColorFidelityMetrics, Opponent notes).`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/exam2/eigen"] = [
  R`<b>Multivariate Gaussian.</b> Zero-mean $x\in\mathbb R^p$ with covariance $R$: $p(x)=\dfrac{1}{(2\pi)^{p/2}|R|^{1/2}}\exp\{-\tfrac12x^tR^{-1}x\}$. $R$ is symmetric positive definite, so $R=E\Lambda E^t$ with $E=[e_1,\dots,e_p]$ orthonormal ($E^tE=EE^t=I$) and $\Lambda=\mathrm{diag}(\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_p)$, $\lambda_k>0$ (EigenSignal notes).`,
  R`<b>Contours.</b> The energy $x^tR^{-1}x$ (squared Mahalanobis distance) has ellipsoidal level sets with principal axes along the eigenvectors $e_k$ and semi-axis lengths proportional to $\sqrt{\lambda_k}$; the contour $x^tR^{-1}x=1$ has semi-axes exactly $\sqrt{\lambda_k}$. In 2-D with $E=\begin{bmatrix}\cos\theta&-\sin\theta\\\sin\theta&\cos\theta\end{bmatrix}$ the major axis (largest $\lambda$) points at angle $\theta$. Sketch: draw $e_1$, mark $\sqrt{\lambda_1}$, $\sqrt{\lambda_2}$.`,
  R`<b>Decorrelation.</b> $\tilde x=E^tx$ has $E[\tilde x\tilde x^t]=E^tRE=E^tE\Lambda E^tE=\Lambda$: uncorrelated components with variances $\lambda_k$, and independent because a linear transform of a Gaussian is Gaussian. The $e_k$ are eigen-signals (eigenimages when $x$ is a vectorised image).`,
  R`<b>Whitening.</b> $W=\Lambda^{-1/2}E^tx$ satisfies $E[WW^t]=\Lambda^{-1/2}E^tRE\Lambda^{-1/2}=I$, so $W\sim N(0,I)$. Always show the covariance computation; that is the proof.`,
  R`<b>Synthesis (generating samples).</b> Start with i.i.d. $N(0,1)$ entries $W$ and set $x=E\Lambda^{1/2}W$: $E[xx^t]=E\Lambda^{1/2}I\Lambda^{1/2}E^t=R$. Any $A$ with $AA^t=R$ works (e.g. the symmetric root $R^{1/2}=E\Lambda^{1/2}E^t$ or a Cholesky factor). The inverse and precision matrix share eigenvectors: $R^{-1}=E\Lambda^{-1}E^t$.`,
  R`<b>Two-step whitening (2026 exam).</b> First normalise variances, $T_1=\mathrm{diag}(R_{11}^{-1/2},R_{22}^{-1/2})$, giving unit variances and correlation $\rho$ (ellipse at $45^\circ$); then rotate by $45^\circ$ with $T_2=\tfrac1{\sqrt2}\begin{bmatrix}1&1\\-1&1\end{bmatrix}$, producing independent components with variances $1+\rho$ and $1-\rho$; finally rescale by $T_3=\mathrm{diag}((1+\rho)^{-1/2},(1-\rho)^{-1/2})$. The product $T_3T_2T_1$ is a whitening matrix.`,
  R`<b>Sample covariance.</b> From $n$ training vectors $X=[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ ($p\times n$): $\hat R=S=\tfrac1nXX^t=\tfrac1n\sum_kx_kx_k^t$, $S_{ij}=\tfrac1n\sum_kX_{ik}X_{jk}$. It is symmetric PSD ($a^tSa=\tfrac1n\|X^ta\|^2\ge0$), unbiased ($E[\hat R]=R$), and has rank $\le\min(p,n)$, so for $n&lt;p$ it is singular with at least $p-n$ zero eigenvalues. Decompose $S=\hat E\hat\Lambda\hat E^t$ to estimate eigenimages.`,
  R`<b>SVD route.</b> $X=U\Sigma V^t$ with $U$ ($p\times n$) and $V$ ($n\times n$) orthonormal-column matrices and $\Sigma$ diagonal, decreasing. Then $XX^t=U\Sigma^2U^t$, so $\hat E=U$ and $\hat\Lambda=\Sigma^2/n$; likewise $\tfrac1pX^tX=V(\Sigma^2/p)V^t$ has eigenvectors $V$, eigenvalues $\Sigma^2/p$. Pitfall: eigenvalues of $\hat R$ are $\sigma_k^2/n$, not $\sigma_k$.`,
  R`<b>Efficient computation when $p\gg n$.</b> Never form the $p\times p$ matrix. Eigen-decompose the small $n\times n$ matrix $X^tX=V\Sigma^2V^t$, then compute $XV=U\Sigma$: the columns of $U$ are the normalised columns of $XV$ and the normalising constants are the singular values. Cost $O(pn^2)$ instead of $O(p^3)$; only $n$ eigenimages are recoverable anyway.`,
  R`<b>Maximum-variance direction.</b> For a unit vector $z$, $E[(z^tx)^2]=z^tRz$ is maximised by the top eigenvector $e_1$ with value $\lambda_1$ (Rayleigh quotient); PCA keeps the first $K$ eigenimages and captures $\sum_{k\le K}\lambda_k/\sum_k\lambda_k$ of the energy. The same decorrelation (KLT) precedes coding of Gaussian vectors (SourceCoding notes).`,
  R`<b>Random vs. deterministic.</b> $R,E,\Lambda$ are constants; $\hat R,\hat E,\hat\Lambda$ and anything computed from training data are random variables. Sign and ordering of eigenvectors are arbitrary: sort by decreasing $\lambda$, and remember $\pm e_k$ are both valid.`,
  R`<b>Checklist for the exam sketch.</b> (1) diagonalise $R$ (2-D: eigenvalues from the characteristic polynomial, eigenvectors unit norm); (2) axes along $e_k$, lengths $\sqrt{\lambda_k}$; (3) decorrelate with $E^t$, whiten with $\Lambda^{-1/2}E^t$; (4) generate with $E\Lambda^{1/2}W$; (5) if asked about $B=R^{-1}$, same eigenvectors, eigenvalues $1/\lambda_k$.`,
  R`<b>Mean and non-zero-mean data.</b> If $E[x]=\mu\ne0$ subtract the sample mean first, otherwise the top "eigenimage" is the mean; the density then uses $(x-\mu)^tR^{-1}(x-\mu)$ and the contours are centred at $\mu$.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/exam2/rp"] = [
  R`<b>Definitions.</b> A discrete random process $X_n$ (or 2-D $X_s$, $s\in\mathbb Z^2$) has mean $\mu_n=E[X_n]$, autocorrelation $R(n,m)=E[X_nX_m]$ and autocovariance $C(n,m)=E[(X_n-\mu_n)(X_m-\mu_m)]$. Second order: these exist. Wide-sense stationary (WSS): $\mu_n$ constant and $C(n+k,n)=C(k)$ depends only on the lag; then $R_x(k)=E[X_nX_{n+k}]$ (RandProc notes).`,
  R`<b>Strict vs. wide sense; a non-stationary counterexample.</b> Strict-sense stationary means all joint distributions are shift invariant; i.i.d. samples are SSS and therefore WSS. Non-example (2024): repeat each $N(0,\sigma^2)$ sample twice, $Y_{2k}=Y_{2k+1}=X_k$: $E[Y_nY_{n+1}]$ equals $\sigma^2$ for even $n$ and $0$ for odd $n$, so the autocorrelation depends on $n$, not stationary, even though every $Y_n$ is $N(0,\sigma^2)$.`,
  R`<b>Power spectral density.</b> $S_x(e^{j\omega})=\lim_{N\to\infty}\tfrac1{2N+1}E\big|\sum_{n=-N}^NX_ne^{-j\omega n}\big|^2$; without the expectation the limit does not converge (the periodogram stays noisy as $N$ grows). Wiener–Khinchin: for WSS $X$, $S_x(e^{j\omega})=\sum_kR_x(k)e^{-j\omega k}$, the DTFT of the autocorrelation; 2-D: $S_x(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})=\sum_{m,n}R(m,n)e^{-j(m\mu+n\nu)}$. Sketch of proof: expand $|X_N|^2$ as a double sum, take expectations to get $R(n-k)$, reindex (WK notes).`,
  R`<b>White noise.</b> $X_n$ i.i.d. $N(0,\sigma^2)$: $R_x(k)=\sigma^2\delta(k)$, $S_x=\sigma^2$ (flat); 2-D $R_x(k,l)=\sigma^2\delta(k,l)$. Variance $=R_x(0)=\tfrac1{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^\pi S_x\,d\omega$, so the PSD is power per unit normalised frequency.`,
  R`<b>Filtering a WSS process.</b> $Y=h*X$ gives $R_y=h*\tilde h*R_x$ with $\tilde h(n)=h(-n)$ and $S_y(e^{j\omega})=|H(e^{j\omega})|^2S_x(e^{j\omega})$; the output mean is $H(e^{j0})\mu_x$. For white input, $R_y(k)=\sigma^2\sum_nh(n)h(n+k)$: the autocorrelation of the impulse response (FiltRandProc notes).`,
  R`<b>FIR examples to know cold.</b> $h=\delta(n)+\delta(n-1)$: $H=1+e^{-j\omega}$, $|H|^2=2+2\cos\omega=4\cos^2(\omega/2)$, $R_y=\sigma^2[2\delta(k)+\delta(k-1)+\delta(k+1)]$. $h=\delta(n)-\delta(n-1)$: $|H|^2=2-2\cos\omega=4\sin^2(\omega/2)$ (high-pass, zero at DC). $h=\delta(n)-\tfrac12\delta(n-1)-\tfrac12\delta(n+1)$: $H=1-\cos\omega$, $S_y=\sigma^2(1-\cos\omega)^2$.`,
  R`<b>First-order recursive system.</b> $Y_n=X_n+aY_{n-1}$, $|a|&lt;1$: $h(n)=a^nu(n)$, $H=\dfrac1{1-ae^{-j\omega}}$, $|H|^2=\dfrac1{1+a^2-2a\cos\omega}$, $S_y=\dfrac{\sigma^2}{1+a^2-2a\cos\omega}$, $R_y(k)=\dfrac{\sigma^2a^{|k|}}{1-a^2}$. For $a>0$ a low-pass peak $\sigma^2/(1-a)^2$ at DC and $\sigma^2/(1+a)^2$ at $\pi$; $a&lt;0$ swaps them (high-pass). Sketch on $[-\pi,\pi]$ with these two values labelled.`,
  R`<b>AR($p$) process.</b> $Y_n=W_n+\sum_{k=1}^ph_kY_{n-k}$ with $W_n$ i.i.d. $N(0,\sigma_w^2)$: $Y(e^{j\omega})=\dfrac{W(e^{j\omega})}{1-H(e^{j\omega})}$, $H=\sum_kh_ke^{-jk\omega}$, so $$S_y(e^{j\omega})=\frac{\sigma_w^2}{|1-\sum_{k=1}^ph_ke^{-jk\omega}|^2}.$$ Stable iff the roots of $z^p-\sum_kh_kz^{p-k}$ lie inside the unit circle; a 1-D least-squares predictor always yields a stable synthesis filter, a 2-D one need not.`,
  R`<b>Causal prediction is exact for AR.</b> $W_n$ is independent of the past $Y_{i&lt;n}$, so $E[Y_n|Y_{i&lt;n}]=\sum_kh_kY_{n-k}$ and the prediction error is $W_n$ itself, variance $\sigma_w^2$. Prediction errors are uncorrelated: for $r&lt;s$, $E[X_sX_r]=E\big[E[X_s|Y_{&lt;s}]X_r\big]=0$ (iterated expectation). For Gaussian processes the MMSE predictor is linear and shift invariant.`,
  R`<b>Analysis: fitting an AR model to data.</b> Regress $y_n$ on $z_n=[y_{n-1},\dots,y_{n-p}]$: $\hat\theta=\hat R^{-1}\hat b$ with $\hat R=\tfrac1N\sum z_n^tz_n$, $\hat b=\tfrac1N\sum z_n^ty_n$ (Yule–Walker with sample correlations); residual $\epsilon_n=y_n-z_n\hat\theta$, $\hat\sigma^2=\tfrac1N\sum\epsilon_n^2$. AR spectral estimate $\hat S=\hat\sigma^2/|1-\hat H|^2$: smooth, unlike the periodogram (FiltRandProc notes).`,
  R`<b>Synthesis: a pseudo-random realisation.</b> Generate i.i.d. $N(0,1)$ numbers $w_n$ (library Gaussian generator or Box–Muller), scale to $\sigma_ww_n$, and run the recursion $y_n=\sigma_ww_n+\sum_kh_ky_{n-k}$ from zero initial conditions, discarding the start-up transient. Output is a "white-noise-driven" process with PSD $\sigma_w^2/|1-H|^2$; in 2-D process pixels in raster order with a causal (quarter-plane or NSHP) predictor.`,
  R`<b>Periodogram averaging.</b> Split $X_n$, $0\le n&lt;NK$, into $K$ blocks of length $N$, $Y^{(k)}_n=X_{kN+n}$, and estimate $\hat S(e^{j\omega})=\tfrac1{NK}\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}|\hat Y^{(k)}(e^{j\omega})|^2$ (2-D: image blocks). More blocks $K$ lower the variance; longer blocks $N$ sharpen frequency resolution (need $N\gg1/$feature width). Fixed data length forces a trade-off (RandProc notes).`,
  R`<b>What is random.</b> $Y=f(X)$ and $\hat X=E[X|Z]$ are random variables; $E[X]$, $R_x(k)$, $S_x$ are deterministic. Useful identities: $E[E[X|Y]]=E[X]$, $E[X|X,Y]=X$, and for jointly Gaussian variables $E[X|Y,Z]=aY+bZ+c$.`,
  R`<b>Two-dimensional notation.</b> $X_s$ with $s=(s_1,s_2)$, $R(m,n)=E[X_{(0,0)}X_{(m,n)}]$, $S_x(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})$; a separable filter gives $S_y=|H_1(e^{j\mu})|^2|H_2(e^{j\nu})|^2S_x$. The separable 2-D AR texture $Y=X+aY(m-1,n)+bY(m,n-1)-abY(m-1,n-1)$ has $S_Y=\sigma^2/(|1-ae^{-j\mu}|^2|1-be^{-j\nu}|^2)$ (see the final-exam RP topic).`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/exam2/gamma"] = [
  R`<b>Display transfer function.</b> A monitor driven with 8-bit value $g$ emits intensity $I=I_0(g/255)^\gamma$ with $\gamma\approx2.2$ (CRT physics; sRGB, NTSC, PCs use 2.2, old Macs 1.8). The stored value is therefore "gamma corrected": $g=255\,(I/I_0)^{1/\gamma}$ (Vision notes).`,
  R`<b>Why gamma is perceptually sensible.</b> Weber: the just-noticeable difference satisfies $\Delta Y_{\rm JND}/Y_B\approx$ const, so lightness with uniform JND steps is $L=\log Y$; correcting for reduced sensitivity in the dark gives the power law $L=Y^p$ with $p=1/\gamma$ ($p=1/3$ fits data, $1/2.2$ is the robust engineering choice). Gamma-corrected values are approximately perceptually uniform.`,
  R`<b>8-bit linear storage fails.</b> Quantising linear intensity to 8 bits gives a step of $1/255$ everywhere; near black (say $Y=2/255$) that step is a 50% contrast change, far above the roughly 1% JND, so smooth dark gradients show false contours (banding), while bright steps are wasted. Store gamma-corrected data as uint8; keep linear-light data in floating point (or 12–16 bits).`,
  R`<b>Checkerboard experiment: derivation.</b> A fine checkerboard of 0 and 255 pixels viewed from a distance is low-pass filtered by the eye, so its perceived intensity is the average $\tfrac12(I_0+0)=I_0/2$ (in linear light). A uniform gray $g_0$ matches when $I_0(g_0/255)^\gamma=I_0/2$, so $$g_0=255\cdot2^{-1/\gamma},\qquad \gamma=\frac{\log 2}{\log(255/g_0)}.$$ For $\gamma=2.2$, $g_0\approx186$; if the match is at 186, $\gamma\approx2.2$. Any pattern with known duty cycle works the same way.`,
  R`<b>Averages must be taken in linear light.</b> Mixing $a$ and $b$ produces intensity $\tfrac12I_0[(a/255)^\gamma+(b/255)^\gamma]$, not the intensity of $(a+b)/2$. Example: a 64/192 checkerboard is brighter than uniform 128 because $\tfrac12(0.25^{2.2}+0.75^{2.2})\approx0.29>0.5^{2.2}\approx0.22$. Hence resize, blur, anti-alias, blend and compute colorimetry after linearising.`,
  R`<b>Camera and display chain.</b> Capture: $x=255(I/I_{\rm in})^{1/\gamma_i}$; display: $\tilde I=I_{\rm out}(x/255)^{\gamma_o}$; end to end $\tilde I=I_{\rm out}(I/I_{\rm in})^{\gamma_o/\gamma_i}$, which is linear only when $\gamma_i=\gamma_o$. Gamma correction is the deliberate choice $\gamma_i=\gamma_o$ so that the two nonlinearities cancel.`,
  R`<b>Processing recipe.</b> uint8 $g$ $\to$ float $y=(g/255)^\gamma$ $\to$ linear operations (filters, matrices $M$, halftoning in absorptance) $\to$ $g'=\mathrm{round}\big(255\,y^{1/\gamma}\big)$ clipped to $[0,255]$ $\to$ uint8. Exact sRGB uses a linear toe: $f(y)=12.92y$ for $y\le0.0031308$, else $1.055y^{1/2.4}-0.055$ (ColorSpaces notes).`,
  R`<b>Which image is which.</b> A linear-intensity image displayed without correction looks too dark and contrasty in the shadows (the display raises it to the 2.2 power); a gamma-corrected image treated as linear looks washed out. Applying gamma twice or in the wrong direction is the classic lab error.`,
  R`<b>Contrast and Weber's law.</b> Contrast $C=(Y-Y_B)/Y_B$; contrast sensitivity $S=1/C_{\rm JND}$ is roughly constant over mid luminances and falls at very low (detector noise, ambient light) and very high (saturation) levels. This is why relative, not absolute, changes matter in image quantisation and compression.`,
  R`<b>Colour and gamma.</b> Gamma is applied per channel to $(r_{\rm lin},g_{\rm lin},b_{\rm lin})$ after white-point scaling; NTSC-style luma $\tilde Y=0.299\tilde r+0.587\tilde g+0.114\tilde b$ is computed on gamma-corrected values by convention and is not true luminance. Equal-energy white and D65 differ, so the gray axis of a display is D65 white, not a flat spectrum.`,
  R`<b>Numbers to remember.</b> $2^{-1/2.2}=0.730$, $0.5^{1/2.2}\approx0.73\Rightarrow186$; $0.5^{2.2}\approx0.218$; $(128/255)^{2.2}\approx0.22$ so mid-gray code 128 is about 22% intensity; 1% JND corresponds to code step $\approx1$ near white but $\approx0.1$ near black in 8-bit linear terms.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/exam2/lpf"] = [
  R`<b>DTFT and its periodicity.</b> $X(e^{j\omega})=\sum_nx(n)e^{-j\omega n}$, $x(n)=\tfrac1{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}X(e^{j\omega})e^{j\omega n}d\omega$. Because $e^{-j(\omega+2\pi)n}=e^{-j\omega n}$, every DTFT is $2\pi$-periodic; the argument $e^{j\omega}$ is written precisely to remind you of that (DTrans notes).`,
  R`<b>Ideal low-pass, valid for all $\omega$.</b> On $[-\pi,\pi]$, $H=\mathrm{rect}\big(\tfrac{\omega}{2\omega_c}\big)$; for all $\omega$, $$H(e^{j\omega})=\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}\mathrm{rect}\Big(\frac{\omega-2\pi k}{2\omega_c}\Big)=\mathrm{prect}_{2\omega_c}(\omega).$$ Writing a single rect and calling it the DTFT loses points. Sketch on $[-3\pi,3\pi]$: unit boxes of width $2\omega_c$ centred at $0,\pm2\pi$ (and the edges of $\pm4\pi$ boxes if $\omega_c$ is large).`,
  R`<b>Impulse response.</b> $h(n)=\tfrac1{2\pi}\int_{-\omega_c}^{\omega_c}e^{j\omega n}d\omega=\dfrac{\sin\omega_cn}{\pi n}=\dfrac{\omega_c}{\pi}\mathrm{sinc}\Big(\dfrac{\omega_c}{\pi}n\Big)$, with $h(0)=\omega_c/\pi$. Zeros at $n=k\pi/\omega_c$; DC gain $\sum_nh(n)=H(e^{j0})=1$; energy $\sum h^2=\omega_c/\pi$ (Parseval).`,
  R`<b>Special cases.</b> $\omega_c=\pi$: boxes tile the axis, $H\equiv1$, $h=\delta(n)$ (all-pass; $\mathrm{sinc}(n)=\delta(n)$ on integers). $\omega_c=\pi/2$: $h(n)=\tfrac12\mathrm{sinc}(n/2)$, so $h(0)=\tfrac12$, $h(\pm1)=1/\pi$, $h(\pm2)=0$, $h(\pm3)=-1/(3\pi)$: sketch a sinc through every other integer.`,
  R`<b>Sampled-sinc viewpoint.</b> $h(n)$ is $T\,\mathrm{sinc}(Tt)\leftrightarrow\mathrm{rect}(f/T)$ sampled at unit rate with $T=\omega_c/\pi$: the sampling theorem $X(e^{j\omega})=\sum_kS\big(\tfrac{\omega-2\pi k}{2\pi}\big)$ produces exactly the periodic rect with cutoff $\omega_c=\pi T$. The same picture explains the final-exam "sampled sinc" problems (Sampling notes).`,
  R`<b>Relation to continuous frequency.</b> If $x(n)=s(nT_s)$ then $\omega=2\pi fT_s$; a cutoff $\omega_c$ corresponds to $f_c=\omega_c/(2\pi T_s)$ Hz. Label axes: $\omega$ in rad/sample, $f$ in Hz or cycles/mm.`,
  R`<b>From low-pass to other filters.</b> High-pass: $\delta(n)-h(n)\leftrightarrow1-H$. Band-pass by modulation: $h(n)\cos\omega_0n\leftrightarrow\tfrac12[H(e^{j(\omega-\omega_0)})+H(e^{j(\omega+\omega_0)})]$. 2-D separable: $\mathrm{prect}(\mu)\mathrm{prect}(\nu)\leftrightarrow h_1(m)h_1(n)$; a circular pass-band gives a jinc, not separable.`,
  R`<b>Realisability and windowing.</b> $h$ is infinite and non-causal. Truncate to $2N+1$ taps and delay by $N$; the rectangular window convolves $H$ with a Dirichlet kernel, giving about 9% Gibbs ripple and $-13$ dB sidelobes that do not shrink with $N$. Tapered windows (Hamming, Kaiser) reduce ripple at the cost of a wider transition band.`,
  R`<b>Multirate uses.</b> Anti-alias filter before down-sampling by $D$: $\omega_c=\pi/D$, gain 1, $h=\tfrac1D\mathrm{sinc}(n/D)$. Interpolation filter after zero-insertion by $L$: $\omega_c=\pi/L$, gain $L$, $h=\mathrm{sinc}(n/L)$ (RateConversion notes).`,
  R`<b>Sinc convolution rule.</b> Cascading two ideal low-passes multiplies the rects: the narrower cutoff wins, $h_1*h_2=h_{\min(\omega_{c1},\omega_{c2})}$. Cascading with its own high-pass complement gives zero.`,
  R`<b>Frequency-response sketch checklist.</b> Mark $\pm\omega_c$, $\pm(2\pi-\omega_c)$, the height (1 for the ideal filter, $L$ for an interpolator), and show at least one full period beyond $\pm\pi$; state whether the plot is $|H|$ or $H$ (the ideal filter is real and even, so phase is zero).`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/exam2/edge"] = [
  R`<b>First differences.</b> Taylor expansion of $s(t)$ around $t=n-\tfrac12$ shows $x(n)-x(n-1)\approx s'(n-\tfrac12)$ (backward), $x(n+1)-x(n)\approx s'(n+\tfrac12)$ (forward), and the central difference $\tfrac12[x(n+1)-x(n-1)]\approx s'(n)$ with $O(h^2)$ error. Impulse responses: $h=\delta(n)-\delta(n-1)$, $\delta(n+1)-\delta(n)$, $\tfrac12[\delta(n+1)-\delta(n-1)]$.`,
  R`<b>Their frequency responses.</b> $1-e^{-j\omega}=2j\sin(\omega/2)e^{-j\omega/2}$: magnitude $2|\sin(\omega/2)|$, zero at DC, maximum 2 at $\pi$, and a half-sample delay (linear phase $-\omega/2$), which is why the estimate lives at $n-\tfrac12$. Central difference: $j\sin\omega$, no delay but zero at $\omega=\pi$ (blind to the finest checkerboard).`,
  R`<b>Second derivative.</b> Forward difference of the backward difference: $x(n+1)-2x(n)+x(n-1)\approx s''(n)$, $h=\delta(n+1)-2\delta(n)+\delta(n-1)$, $H(e^{j\omega})=2\cos\omega-2=-4\sin^2(\omega/2)$: real, even, zero at DC, $-4$ at $\pi$. The separable 2-D version $\tfrac1{16}[1,-2,1]^t[1,-2,1]$ has $H=\tfrac14(1-\cos\mu)(1-\cos\nu)$ with $H(0,0)=0$ (Filters notes).`,
  R`<b>Edge signature.</b> Model an edge as a blurred step (a ramp). The first derivative peaks at the edge centre; the second derivative crosses zero there, positive on one side and negative on the other. Both facts localise the edge to one sample.`,
  R`<b>Combined edge test.</b> Declare an edge at $n$ if $|d_1(n)|>T$ <em>and</em> $d_2$ changes sign in the neighbourhood ($d_2(n-1)d_2(n)&lt;0$ or $d_2(n)d_2(n+1)&lt;0$). The gradient threshold rejects zero crossings caused by noise in flat regions; the zero crossing rejects wide ramps and thins the response to a single location (the gradient maximum). Align the half-sample offset of $[1,-1]$ consistently.`,
  R`<b>Noise amplification.</b> Difference filters are high-pass, so white noise of variance $\sigma^2$ becomes $\sigma^2\sum h^2$: $2\sigma^2$ for $[1,-1]$, $6\sigma^2$ for $[1,-2,1]$, and the noise spectrum is weighted by $|H|^2$, which grows toward $\pi$. Smooth first: Gaussian then derivative equals derivative-of-Gaussian; Gaussian then Laplacian is the Laplacian of Gaussian, whose zero crossings are the Marr–Hildreth edges. Larger $\sigma$ means fewer, coarser edges.`,
  R`<b>2-D derivative filters.</b> The notes' horizontal derivative $\tfrac1{16}\begin{bmatrix}2&0&-2\\4&0&-4\\2&0&-2\end{bmatrix}$ is separable: vertical smoothing $\tfrac14[1,2,1]^t$ times the central difference $\tfrac12[1,0,-1]$ (a Sobel-type kernel). Gradient magnitude $\sqrt{g_x^2+g_y^2}$ and direction $\arctan(g_y/g_x)$; the edge is perpendicular to the gradient.`,
  R`<b>Laplacian.</b> $\nabla^2f\approx f(m+1,n)+f(m-1,n)+f(m,n+1)+f(m,n-1)-4f(m,n)$, DSFT $2\cos\mu+2\cos\nu-4$, isotropic to second order, zero DC gain. Its zero crossings give closed edge contours but are noise-sensitive; subtracting a multiple of it is unsharp masking (see the final LTI topic).`,
  R`<b>Convolution index conventions.</b> With $y(n)=\sum_kh(k)x(n-k)$, the "kernel" $[1,-1]$ meaning $h(0)=1,h(1)=-1$ computes $x(n)-x(n-1)$; if a problem writes a mask to be laid over the image (correlation), flip it before calling it $h$. Symmetric kernels such as $[1,-2,1]$ are unaffected.`,
  R`<b>Boundary handling.</b> Free boundary condition: pixels outside the image are zero, which manufactures strong false edges along the border (the notes' worked examples show this); alternatives are replication or reflection.`,
  R`<b>Frequency sketches.</b> $|1-e^{-j\omega}|=2|\sin(\omega/2)|$ rises from 0 to 2; $|2\cos\omega-2|=4\sin^2(\omega/2)$ from 0 to 4; both $2\pi$-periodic. A derivative estimator must have $H(e^{j0})=0$ (constant input gives no edge) and $H\approx j\omega$ near DC.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/exam2/samp2"] = [
  R`<b>Linear coordinate change (the key theorem).</b> If $f(\mathbf x)=\tilde f(A\mathbf x)$ with $A$ invertible, then $$F(\mathbf u)=\frac{1}{|\det A|}\tilde F(A^{-t}\mathbf u).$$ Proof sketch: substitute $\mathbf x'=A\mathbf x$, $d\mathbf x=d\mathbf x'/|\det A|$, and write $\mathbf u^t A^{-1}\mathbf x'=(A^{-t}\mathbf u)^t\mathbf x'$ (CSFT notes).`,
  R`<b>Rotation and scaling.</b> For $A=cB$ with $B$ orthonormal, $A^{-t}=B/c$ and $|\det A|=c^2$, so $F(\mathbf u)=\tfrac1{c^2}\tilde F(B\mathbf u/c)$: the spectrum rotates by the same rotation and is stretched by $c$ while $f$ is compressed by $c$. A pure rotation leaves $|\det|=1$: $g(A\mathbf x)\leftrightarrow G(A\mathbf u)$.`,
  R`<b>Support mapping.</b> $F(\mathbf u)\ne0$ only where $A^{-t}\mathbf u\in\mathrm{supp}\tilde F$, i.e. $\mathbf u\in A^t\,\mathrm{supp}\tilde F$. A disk of radius $\omega_c$ becomes a disk of radius $c\omega_c$ under $A=cB$; a rectangle becomes a parallelogram. The band limit for $f$ is the largest $|u|$ and $|v|$ of that transformed region.`,
  R`<b>Rectangular sampling of a warped image.</b> Sampling $f$ on the grid $(mT,nT)$ is the same as sampling $\tilde f$ on the lattice $\{TA(m,n)^t\}$: a rotated and scaled grid. The sampled spectrum is $\tfrac1{T^2}\sum_{k,l}F(u-\tfrac kT,v-\tfrac lT)$, so no aliasing iff $\mathrm{supp}F\subset(-\tfrac1{2T},\tfrac1{2T})^2$: for $A=cB$ and a circular band limit, $T&lt;\dfrac1{2c\omega_c}$ (Sampling notes).`,
  R`<b>General lattices.</b> Sampling on $\{V\mathbf n\}$ (columns of $V$ are the lattice vectors, density $1/|\det V|$ samples per unit area) replicates the spectrum on the reciprocal lattice $\{V^{-t}\mathbf k\}$ (cycles) or $\{2\pi V^{-t}\mathbf k\}$ (radians). Rectangular sampling is $V=TI$.`,
  R`<b>Hexagonal sampling.</b> For circularly band-limited images the hexagonal reciprocal lattice packs the spectral replicas most tightly, so about 13% fewer samples suffice than with a square grid at the same alias-free bandwidth.`,
  R`<b>Sampling theorem recap.</b> $s(m,n)=g(mT,nT)\Rightarrow S(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})=\tfrac1{T^2}\sum_{k,l}G\big(\tfrac{\mu-2\pi k}{2\pi T},\tfrac{\nu-2\pi l}{2\pi T}\big)$ with $\mu=2\pi uT$; comb/rep form $\mathrm{comb}_Tg\leftrightarrow\tfrac1{T^2}\mathrm{rep}_{1/T}G$. Reconstruction under Nyquist: $G(u,v)=T^2S(e^{j2\pi uT},e^{j2\pi vT})\mathrm{rect}(uT,vT)$, i.e. $g=\sum s(m,n)\mathrm{sinc}(\tfrac{x-mT}T,\tfrac{y-nT}T)$.`,
  R`<b>Detector + display chain (2025 version).</b> Focal-plane cell integrates over $T\times T$: convolution with $\tfrac1{T^2}\mathrm{rect}(x/T,y/T)$, CSFT $\mathrm{sinc}(Tu,Tv)$, then sampling. Display with square pixels $\mathrm{rect}(x/T,y/T)$ multiplies by $T^2\mathrm{sinc}(Tu,Tv)$. With no aliasing the end-to-end result is $F(u,v)=\mathrm{sinc}^2(Tu,Tv)G(u,v)$ in the base band plus attenuated replicas: a blur of the fine detail.`,
  R`<b>Compensation filter.</b> A digital pre-filter $H_c(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})=1/\mathrm{sinc}^2\big(\tfrac\mu{2\pi},\tfrac\nu{2\pi}\big)$ on $[-\pi,\pi]^2$ flattens the product; the boost at the band edge is $1/\mathrm{sinc}^2(\tfrac12)=\pi^2/4\approx2.47$, so it also amplifies high-frequency noise. Outside $[-\pi,\pi]^2$ the filter is periodic by definition.`,
  R`<b>Aliased plane waves.</b> A wave at $(u_0,v_0)$ sampled with period $T$ appears at $(2\pi u_0T,2\pi v_0T)$ folded into $[-\pi,\pi]^2$; rotation by $A$ moves $(u_0,v_0)$ to $A^t(u_0,v_0)$ first. Under-sampling changes both the frequency and the orientation of the stripes (moiré).`,
  R`<b>Direction pitfalls.</b> $f(\mathbf x)=\tilde f(c\mathbf x)$ with $c>1$ is a <em>smaller</em> picture with a <em>wider</em> spectrum of height $1/c^2$; for a rotation $B$, $f=\tilde f(B\mathbf x)$ is $\tilde f$ rotated by $B^{-1}$ and its spectrum $\tilde F(B\mathbf u)$ is rotated the same way. Check with a plane wave: $\cos(2\pi\mathbf u_0^tA\mathbf x)$ has frequency $A^t\mathbf u_0$.`,
  R`<b>Units.</b> $x,y$ in mm, $u,v$ in cycles/mm, $T$ in mm/sample, $\mu,\nu$ in rad/sample with $\mu=2\pi uT$; $\det A$ is dimensionless for a pure warp. Label the replica spacing $1/T$ (or $2\pi$) on every sketch.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/exam2/mmse"] = [
  R`<b>Setup.</b> Estimate a scalar $X$ (a clean pixel) from a vector $Z\in\mathbb R^p$ (a window of measured pixels, the notes' row vector $z_s$) with a linear estimator $\hat X=\theta^tZ$. Goal: choose $\theta$ to minimise $\mathrm{MSE}(\theta)=E[(X-\theta^tZ)^2]$ (OptLinFilter notes).`,
  R`<b>Expanding the MSE.</b> $E[(X-\theta^tZ)^2]=E[X^2]-2\theta^tE[ZX]+\theta^tE[ZZ^t]\theta=a-2\theta^tb+\theta^tR\theta$ with $a=E[X^2]$, $b=E[ZX]$ ($p\times1$), $R=E[ZZ^t]$ ($p\times p$, symmetric PSD). A convex quadratic in $\theta$.`,
  R`<b>Minimisation.</b> $\nabla_\theta(a-2\theta^tb+\theta^tR\theta)=-2b+2R\theta=0\Rightarrow\theta^\ast=R^{-1}b$ (assuming $R$ invertible). Minimum value $\mathrm{MSE}(\theta^\ast)=a-b^tR^{-1}b$. Remember $\nabla_\theta\theta^tR\theta=2R\theta$ only because $R$ is symmetric.`,
  R`<b>Orthogonality principle.</b> At the optimum $E[(X-\theta^{\ast t}Z)Z]=b-R\theta^\ast=0$: the estimation error is uncorrelated with every component of the data. Geometrically $\hat X$ is the projection of $X$ onto the span of $Z_1,\dots,Z_p$ in the inner product $E[\cdot\,\cdot]$.`,
  R`<b>Sample estimates from training data.</b> Given $N$ pairs $(z_k,x_k)$: $\hat R=\tfrac1N\sum_kz_kz_k^t=\tfrac1NZ^tZ$, $\hat b=\tfrac1N\sum_kz_kx_k=\tfrac1NZ^tX$, $\hat a=\tfrac1N\sum x_k^2$, and $\hat\theta=\hat R^{-1}\hat b=(Z^tZ)^{-1}Z^tX$. This is exactly least squares: $\hat\theta=\arg\min_\theta\|X-Z\theta\|^2$, and the fitted values $\hat X=Z\hat\theta$ are the projection of $X$ onto the columns of $Z$.`,
  R`<b>Which quantities are random.</b> $a,b,R,\theta^\ast$ and $\mathrm{MSE}(\theta)$ are deterministic numbers determined by the joint distribution; $\hat a,\hat b,\hat R,\hat\theta$, the training loss and $\hat X$ are random variables (functions of the training data). $E[\hat R]=R$ and $E[\hat b]=b$, but $E[\hat\theta]\ne\theta^\ast$ in general because inversion is nonlinear; $\hat\theta\to\theta^\ast$ as $N\to\infty$ by the law of large numbers.`,
  R`<b>Training vs. testing.</b> Training performance is always optimistic ($\hat\theta$ was chosen to minimise it); evaluate on held-out pairs. $N$ must be much larger than $p$ or $\hat R$ is singular or ill-conditioned and $\hat\theta$ overfits; both training and test error approach the optimum as $N$ grows.`,
  R`<b>Gaussian optimality.</b> If $(X,Z)$ are jointly Gaussian then $E[X|Z]$ is affine in $Z$, so the MMSE linear (Wiener) filter is the best possible estimator. For real images (non-Gaussian) nonlinear estimators can beat it; the linear filter remains useful but blurs edges and handles impulsive noise poorly.`,
  R`<b>Any quadratic norm gives the same answer.</b> With an HVS weighting $\|X-\hat X\|_B^2$, $B=H^tH$, the minimiser is still $E[X|Y]$ (set $V=HX$), and the best linear filter under this norm is the same MMSE linear filter; so weighting does not change the design, only the evaluation (OptLinFilter notes).`,
  R`<b>Affine term and mean.</b> To allow a constant offset, append 1 to $Z$ (so $\theta$ gains a bias); for zero-mean data it is unnecessary. If the window is space invariant, $\theta$ is an FIR filter and the estimator is an LSI filter applied to the whole image.`,
  R`<b>Connections.</b> AR prediction is this problem with $Z$ = the $p$ previous samples: $b=[R(1),\dots,R(p)]^t$, $R$ Toeplitz (Yule–Walker). Deblurring/denoising filter design (final-exam LS topic) is the same with $Z$ = a neighbourhood of the corrupted image and $X$ = the clean pixel.`,
  R`<b>Bookkeeping pitfalls.</b> Keep the notes' shapes: $z_s$ row, $\theta$ column, $\hat x_s=z_s\theta$, $Z$ is $N\times p$; the $1/N$ factors cancel in $\hat\theta$ but not in the losses; state assumptions ($R$ invertible, zero mean) explicitly.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/exam2/hvs"] = [
  R`<b>The eye as a camera.</b> Retina = focal plane array; fovea $\approx1^\circ$ of high resolution; rods for scotopic (dark, achromatic) vision, L, M, S cones for photopic colour vision; blind spot at the optic nerve. Luminance $Y=\int I(\lambda)y(\lambda)d\lambda$ is proportional to energy weighted by the photopic sensitivity (400–700 nm) (Vision notes).`,
  R`<b>Display geometry.</b> A 16:9 screen with diagonal $D$ has width $W=D\cdot\dfrac{16}{\sqrt{16^2+9^2}}=D\cdot\dfrac{16}{18.36}\approx0.872D$ and height $\approx0.490D$. Pixels per inch: ppi $=$ horizontal pixel count $/W$ (inches).`,
  R`<b>Pixels per degree.</b> At viewing distance $d$, one degree subtends $d\tan1^\circ\approx d\cdot\pi/180$, so $\mathrm{ppd}\approx\mathrm{ppi}\times d\times\pi/180$ with $d$ in inches. Example: 65-inch 4K TV, $W=56.7$ in, ppi $=3840/56.7=67.7$; at 10 ft ($120$ in) ppd $\approx67.7\times120\times0.01745\approx142$.`,
  R`<b>Is the pixel grid visible?</b> A one-pixel checkerboard has spatial frequency $\mathrm{ppd}/2$ cycles/degree. Human contrast sensitivity peaks near 3–5 cpd and falls to zero at roughly 50–60 cpd, so the grid disappears when $\mathrm{ppd}/2$ exceeds about 60, i.e. ppd $\gtrsim120$. In the example, 71 cpd is above the cutoff: invisible.`,
  R`<b>Contrast sensitivity function.</b> The luminance channel is band-pass (insensitive to the mean level, sharp at mid frequencies); the red–green and blue–yellow opponent channels are low-pass with lower cutoffs. Consequences: chroma may be subsampled 2:1, analog video gives I and Q less bandwidth, isoluminant patterns look blurry, halftone noise must be pushed to high frequencies (Opponent notes).`,
  R`<b>Weber's law.</b> Contrast $C=\Delta Y/Y_B$; the JND contrast $C_{\rm JND}\approx1$–2% is roughly independent of background luminance over the mid range, failing at very dark (noise) and very bright (saturation) levels. It motivates $L=\log Y$ and the power-law lightness $L=Y^{1/\gamma}$ (see the gamma topic).`,
  R`<b>Incoherent light adds in power.</b> Let two sources produce complex fields $e_0,e_1$ with independent, uniformly random phases (zero mean). Total intensity $E|e_0+e_1|^2=E|e_0|^2+E|e_1|^2+2\,\mathrm{Re}\,E[e_0e_1^*]$, and independence with zero mean kills the cross term, so intensities (powers) add. This is why brightness from lamps, phosphors and LEDs is additive (Grassmann's law needs it).`,
  R`<b>Coherent light interferes.</b> With a fixed phase relation (laser, single source split in two), $|e_0+e_1|^2=|e_0|^2+|e_1|^2+2|e_0||e_1|\cos\Delta\phi$: amplitudes add, producing fringes, speckle and holography. Coherence is the exception in everyday imaging.`,
  R`<b>Opponent colour paradox.</b> $O_1$ luminance, $O_2=G-R$, $O_3=B-Y$ with non-orthogonal analysis vectors; blue text on yellow paper is legible because that stimulus is far from isoluminant (blue is dark), so it drives the sharp $O_1$ channel; yellow text on white or blue on black couples mainly to the blurry $O_3$ channel and is hard to read.`,
  R`<b>Unit hygiene.</b> Convert distance to the same unit as ppi, degrees to radians ($\pi/180$), use the width not the diagonal, and quote cycles/degree (half the pixels/degree) for a checkerboard. Show the small-angle approximation explicitly.`,
  R`<b>Lightness and Lab.</b> Perceptual uniformity: $L^\ast=116f(Y/Y_0)-16$ with $f\approx(\cdot)^{1/3}$, so equal steps in $L^\ast$ are equally visible; Lab is designed for large patches (paint, printer calibration), not for pixel-level image error, because it ignores the spatial CSF (ColorFidelityMetrics notes).`
];

/* ===================== FINAL ===================== */

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/final/samp"] = [
  R`<b>Core transform pairs.</b> $\mathrm{sinc}(t)=\dfrac{\sin\pi t}{\pi t}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{rect}(f)$, $\mathrm{rect}(t)\leftrightarrow\mathrm{sinc}(f)$, $\mathrm{sinc}(t/\tau)\leftrightarrow\tau\,\mathrm{rect}(\tau f)$, $\Lambda(t)\leftrightarrow\mathrm{sinc}^2(f)$; comb/rep: $\mathrm{comb}_T[x]\leftrightarrow\tfrac1T\mathrm{rep}_{1/T}[X]$ (CTFT, RepComb notes).`,
  R`<b>Sampling theorem.</b> $x(n)=s(nT)\Rightarrow X(e^{j\omega})=\dfrac1T\sum_kS\Big(\dfrac{\omega-2\pi k}{2\pi T}\Big)$, $\omega=2\pi fT$, $f_s=1/T$. Alias-free iff $S(f)=0$ for $|f|\ge\tfrac1{2T}$, i.e. $T&lt;\tfrac1{2f_c}$ (Nyquist period $1/(2f_c)$). Sketch: replicas spaced $2\pi$, height scaled by $1/T$, width scaled by $2\pi T$.`,
  R`<b>Sampled sinc worked example.</b> $s(t)=\mathrm{sinc}(t)$, $f_c=\tfrac12$, Nyquist $T=1$. $T=0.1$: $x(n)=\mathrm{sinc}(0.1n)$, $X=10\sum_k\mathrm{rect}\big(\tfrac{\omega-2\pi k}{0.2\pi}\big)$ (tall narrow boxes, $|\omega|&lt;0.1\pi$). $T=0.5$: $x(n)=\mathrm{sinc}(n/2)$, $X=2\sum_k\mathrm{rect}\big(\tfrac{\omega-2\pi k}{\pi}\big)$. $T=1$: $x(n)=\delta(n)$, $X\equiv1$: the boxes exactly tile. Label $\omega$ in rad/sample, heights $1/T$.`,
  R`<b>Reconstruction.</b> Under Nyquist, $S(f)=T\,X(e^{j2\pi fT})\,\mathrm{rect}(fT)$ and $s(t)=\sum_nx(n)\,\mathrm{sinc}\big(\tfrac{t-nT}{T}\big)$: an ideal low-pass with gain $T$ and cutoff $1/(2T)$ applied to the impulse train. In the example with $T=1$, $x=\delta$ reconstructs $\mathrm{sinc}(t)$ exactly.`,
  R`<b>Up-sampling (zero insertion).</b> $z(n)=x(n/L)$ when $L\mid n$, else 0; equivalently $z(n)=\sum_mx(m)\delta(n-mL)$. Then $Z(e^{j\omega})=X(e^{j\omega L})$: the spectrum compresses by $L$ and $L-1$ images appear in $[-\pi,\pi]$. Linear but not time invariant (a shift by one moves the zeros).`,
  R`<b>Interpolation filter.</b> After zero insertion apply $H(e^{j\omega})=L\,\mathrm{prect}_{2\pi/L}(\omega)$ ($=L$ for $|\omega|&lt;\pi/L$), $h(n)=\mathrm{sinc}(n/L)$; the gain $L$ restores the amplitude lost to zero insertion and the output is $y(n)=s(nT/L)$. Cheap substitutes: pixel replication $h=$ indicator of $[0,L-1]^2$, bilinear $h=\Lambda(m/L)\Lambda(n/L)$; the sinc has infinite support and negative sidelobes (ringing) (RateConversion notes).`,
  R`<b>Down-sampling and decimation.</b> $y(n)=x(Dn)\Rightarrow Y(e^{j\omega})=\tfrac1D\sum_{k=0}^{D-1}X(e^{j(\omega-2\pi k)/D})$: frequencies above $\pi/D$ alias. Decimation = anti-alias filter $H=\mathrm{prect}_{2\pi/D}$, $h=\tfrac1D\mathrm{sinc}(n/D)$, then subsample; 2-D $h=\tfrac1{D^2}\mathrm{sinc}(m/D)\mathrm{sinc}(n/D)$. Block averaging is a cheap approximation with some aliasing; direct subsampling aliases badly.`,
  R`<b>Multirate systems: LTI or not?</b> Down-samplers and up-samplers are linear but not shift invariant. Up by $L$, filter, down by $L$ can be LTI (e.g. with an ideal filter of cutoff $\pi/L$ it reduces to an LTI filter, or to identity); down then up is not (it zeroes samples). Test shift invariance on $\delta(n)$ vs $\delta(n-1)$, or compute $y$ for a general $x$ and check for dependence on $n\bmod L$.`,
  R`<b>Focal-plane array model.</b> Each cell integrates over $T\times T$: $s(m,n)=\iint\tfrac1{T^2}\mathrm{rect}(\tfrac{x-mT}T,\tfrac{y-nT}T)g\,dxdy$, i.e. convolution with $\tfrac1{T^2}\mathrm{rect}(x/T,y/T)$ (CSFT $\mathrm{sinc}(Tu,Tv)$) followed by sampling: $S(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})=\tfrac1{T^2}\sum_{k,l}\mathrm{sinc}(\mu/2\pi-k,\nu/2\pi-l)\,G(\tfrac{\mu-2\pi k}{2\pi T},\tfrac{\nu-2\pi l}{2\pi T})$ (Sampling notes).`,
  R`<b>Display model and full chain.</b> An LCD renders $f(x,y)=\sum s(m,n)\mathrm{rect}(\tfrac{x-mT}T,\tfrac{y-nT}T)$, so $F(u,v)=T^2\mathrm{sinc}(Tu,Tv)\,S(e^{j2\pi uT},e^{j2\pi vT})$. With no aliasing, $F(u,v)=\mathrm{sinc}^2(Tu,Tv)G(u,v)$ in the base band plus attenuated replicas at multiples of $1/T$. Compensation: digital filter $1/\mathrm{sinc}^2(\tfrac\mu{2\pi},\tfrac\nu{2\pi})$ on $[-\pi,\pi]^2$ (gain $\pi^2/4$ at the band edge, amplifies noise).`,
  R`<b>Two-dimensional sampling theorem.</b> $s(m,n)=g(mT_x,nT_y)\Rightarrow S=\tfrac1{T_xT_y}\sum_{k,l}G\big(\tfrac{\mu-2\pi k}{2\pi T_x},\tfrac{\nu-2\pi l}{2\pi T_y}\big)$; a plane wave at $(u_0,v_0)$ lands at $(2\pi u_0T_x,2\pi v_0T_y)$ folded into $[-\pi,\pi]^2$; non-rectangular grids via $f(\mathbf x)=\tilde f(A\mathbf x)\leftrightarrow\tfrac1{|\det A|}\tilde F(A^{-t}\mathbf u)$ (Exam 2 sampling topic).`,
  R`<b>Continuous-time viewpoint of rate change.</b> Up-sampling by $L$ then ideal interpolation equals resampling the underlying band-limited $s(t)$ at $T/L$; decimation by $D$ equals low-pass filtering $s(t)$ to $\tfrac1{2DT}$ and resampling at $DT$. Use this to predict spectra without index gymnastics.`,
  R`<b>Ideal DT low-pass reminder.</b> $h(n)=\tfrac{\omega_c}{\pi}\mathrm{sinc}(\tfrac{\omega_c}{\pi}n)$; $\omega_c=\pi$ gives $\delta(n)$; write $H$ as a periodic rep of rects when asked for all $\omega$ (Exam 2 LPF topic).`,
  R`<b>Units and labelling.</b> $T$ in seconds or mm, $f_s$ in Hz or cycles/mm, $\omega,\mu,\nu$ in rad/sample, spectral heights carry $1/T$ or $1/T^2$; always mark the Nyquist edge $\pm\pi$ and the replica period.`,
  R`<b>Common mistakes.</b> Forgetting the $1/T$ amplitude factor; drawing only one period of a DTFT; using cutoff $\pi/L$ with gain 1 for interpolation (output is $L$ times too small); claiming a down-sampler is time invariant; confusing $\mathrm{sinc}(t)=\sin(\pi t)/(\pi t)$ with $\sin t/t$.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/final/ct"] = [
  R`<b>Beer–Lambert.</b> $\dfrac{d\lambda_x}{dx}=-\mu(x)\lambda_x\Rightarrow\lambda_x=\lambda_0\exp\{-\int_0^x\mu(t)dt\}$; the blank (air) scan measures $\lambda_0$ (dose, gains, exposure) and the object scan $\lambda_T$, so the projection is $p=\int_0^T\mu=-\log(\lambda_T/\lambda_0)$. Counts $Y_T$ are Poisson with mean and variance $\lambda_T$ (Exam 1 CT topic; Tomography notes).`,
  R`<b>Path length through a known material.</b> With $\mu(x)=\mu_0I(x)$ ($I$ the indicator of the object), $L=\int I=-\dfrac1{\mu_0}\log\dfrac{y_T}{y_{T,0}}$ and $y_T=y_0e^{-\mu_0L}$, which falls to $y_0/e$ at $L=1/\mu_0$: 4.76 cm at 0.21 cm$^{-1}$ (40 keV polyethylene) vs 5.88 cm at 0.17 cm$^{-1}$ (80 keV). Sketch two decaying exponentials from $y_0$, the higher energy decaying more slowly.`,
  R`<b>Beam hardening.</b> For a source with energy fractions $w_e$ ($\sum w_e=1$) and attenuations $\mu_e$: $y_T=y_0\sum_ew_ee^{-\mu_eL}$, so $p(L)=-\log\sum_ew_ee^{-\mu_eL}$. Then $p(0)=0$, $p'(L)=\dfrac{\sum w_e\mu_ee^{-\mu_eL}}{\sum w_ee^{-\mu_eL}}$ is a weighted mean of the $\mu_e$ that decreases from $\bar\mu=\sum w_e\mu_e$ toward $\min_e\mu_e$ as low-energy photons are absorbed, hence $p$ is concave, not linear in $L$: thick paths are under-estimated (cupping, streaks between dense objects). Fix by a polynomial linearisation of $p(L)$, pre-filtering the beam, or dual-energy modelling.`,
  R`<b>Preprocessing pipeline.</b> (1) blank scan $y_0$ and dark/offset correction; (2) object scan $y$; (3) transmission $y/y_0\in(0,1]$; (4) sinogram $p_\theta(r)=-\log(y/y_0)$ (divide by $\mu_0$ for path length); (5) beam-hardening and detector-gain corrections; (6) filtered back-projection. Zero counts make $-\log0$ undefined: clamp or use a statistical reconstruction.`,
  R`<b>Radon transform.</b> $p_\theta(r)=\iint f(x,y)\,\delta(r-x\cos\theta-y\sin\theta)\,dxdy$: the line integral along the line at angle $\theta$ and offset $r$; equivalently rotate coordinates so $r=x\cos\theta+y\sin\theta$, $z=-x\sin\theta+y\cos\theta$ and integrate over $z$. $\theta\in[0,\pi)$ suffices because $p_{\theta+\pi}(r)=p_\theta(-r)$.`,
  R`<b>Fourier slice theorem.</b> $P_\theta(\rho)=\int p_\theta(r)e^{-j2\pi\rho r}dr=F(\rho\cos\theta,\rho\sin\theta)$: the 1-D CTFT of a projection is the slice of the 2-D CSFT through the origin at angle $\theta$. Proof: substitute the definition of $p_\theta$, swap integrals, and recognise $\iint f(x,y)e^{-j2\pi\rho(x\cos\theta+y\sin\theta)}dxdy$. Corollary (band-limit property): if $F=0$ outside a disk of radius $\rho_c$, every projection is band-limited to $\rho_c$, so detectors may be spaced $T_d\le1/(2\rho_c)$.`,
  R`<b>Convolution back-projection derivation.</b> Write the inverse CSFT in polar coordinates, $du\,dv=|\rho|\,d\rho\,d\theta$: $$f(x,y)=\int_0^\pi\!\!\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}|\rho|P_\theta(\rho)e^{j2\pi\rho(x\cos\theta+y\sin\theta)}d\rho\,d\theta=\int_0^\pi g_\theta(x\cos\theta+y\sin\theta)\,d\theta,$$ with filtered projections $g_\theta=h*p_\theta$, $H(\rho)=|\rho|$ (ramp). Algorithm: measure $p_\theta$, filter, back-project (smear $g_\theta(r)$ along lines of angle $\theta$) and sum over angles, $\approx\tfrac\pi M\sum_mb_{m\pi/M}(x,y)$.`,
  R`<b>Practical ramp filter.</b> Band-limit to $|\rho|\le f_c$: $H(\rho)=f_c[\mathrm{rect}(\rho/2f_c)-\Lambda(\rho/f_c)]$, $h(r)=f_c^2[2\mathrm{sinc}(2f_cr)-\mathrm{sinc}^2(f_cr)]$. With detector pitch $T_d$ and $f_c=1/(2T_d)$ the discrete filter is $h(n)=\tfrac1{T_d}\big[\tfrac14\delta(n)-\tfrac14\mathrm{sinc}^2(n/2)\big]$: $\tfrac1{4T_d}$ at $n=0$, 0 for even $n\ne0$, $-\tfrac1{T_d\pi^2n^2}$ for odd $n$. Its DC gain is zero, so plain back-projection would leave a positive bias (Tomography notes).`,
  R`<b>Unfiltered back-projection blurs with $1/r$.</b> Back-projecting the raw $p_\theta$ gives $b(x,y)=\int_0^\pi p_\theta(x\cos\theta+y\sin\theta)d\theta=f*\dfrac1{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}$ (each point spreads uniformly along all lines through it, density $\propto1/r$). Since the CSFT of $1/r$ is $1/\rho$ (circularly symmetric; check via $\int e^{j2\pi rt}dr=\delta(t)$ and $dxdy=r\,dr\,d\theta$), $B(u,v)=F(u,v)/\rho$, and multiplying by $|\rho|$ restores $F$: FBP is deconvolution of the $1/r$ blur. It is benign because $1/\rho$ has no zeros, unlike a sinc-shaped blur, which cannot be inverted where it vanishes (2023 final).`,
  R`<b>Sampling of the sinogram.</b> Detector spacing set by the band limit, $T_d\le1/(2\rho_c)$; angular spacing so that the outermost sample spacing $\pi R\Delta\theta/\pi\cdot$ is comparable, roughly $M\approx\pi N/2$ views for $N$ detectors. Under-sampling angles gives streaks radiating from sharp objects.`,
  R`<b>Noise propagation.</b> $\mathrm{Var}(\hat p)\approx1/\lambda_T=e^{p}/\lambda_0$: noise grows exponentially with attenuation, so rays through metal or bone dominate and the ramp filter amplifies high-frequency noise, producing streaks. Remedies: more dose, apodised ramp (Hann/Shepp–Logan), or model-based iterative reconstruction with a Poisson likelihood.`,
  R`<b>Photon energy and $\mu(E)$.</b> $E=h\nu=hc/\lambda_{\rm w}$; $\mu$ generally decreases with energy (photoelectric $\propto E^{-3}$, Compton nearly flat), which is what makes a polychromatic beam harden. Two-energy problems: draw $y_T(L)$ for each energy and their weighted sum.`,
  R`<b>Emission tomography.</b> PET/SPECT: unknown emission rates $x_j$, $E[y_i]=\sum_jA_{ij}x_j$ linear in the unknown (no log), still Poisson; coincidence detection defines the line. Same Radon geometry and FBP apply after attenuation correction.`,
  R`<b>Exam habits.</b> Show the ODE and its solution, state where the blank scan enters, keep units ($\mu$ in cm$^{-1}$, $L$ in cm, $p$ dimensionless), and when proving concavity argue via the derivative (or Jensen: $-\log$ of a convex combination of exponentials).`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/final/ls"] = [
  R`<b>Two estimators, one form.</b> A linear filter $\hat X=\theta^tZ$ (scalar) or $\hat X=Z\theta$ (a training set: rows $z_s$ are windows of the corrupted image, $X$ the clean pixels). Least squares picks $\theta$ from data; MMSE picks $\theta$ from the joint distribution. Know both derivations, their solutions and which quantities are random (OptLinFilter notes).`,
  R`<b>Least-squares loss.</b> $L(\theta)=\tfrac1N\|X-Z\theta\|^2=\tfrac1N(X^tX-2\theta^tZ^tX+\theta^tZ^tZ\theta)=\hat a-2\hat b^t\theta+\theta^t\hat R\theta$ with $\hat R=\tfrac1NZ^tZ$, $\hat b=\tfrac1NZ^tX$, $\hat a=\tfrac1NX^tX$. Gradient $-2\hat b+2\hat R\theta=0$ gives $\hat\theta=\hat R^{-1}\hat b=(Z^tZ)^{-1}Z^tX$; the fitted values $\hat X=Z(Z^tZ)^{-1}Z^tX$ are the orthogonal projection of $X$ onto the column space of $Z$, and the residual $X-\hat X$ is orthogonal to every column.`,
  R`<b>Expected loss.</b> $\bar L(\theta)=E[L(\theta)]=E[(x_s-z_s\theta)^2]=a-2b^t\theta+\theta^tR\theta$ with $a=E[x_s^2]$, $b=E[z_s^tx_s]$, $R=E[z_s^tz_s]$; minimiser $\theta^\ast=R^{-1}b$, minimum $\bar L(\theta^\ast)=a-b^tR^{-1}b$. Since $E[\hat R]=R$, $E[\hat b]=b$, $E[\hat a]=a$, the LS loss is an unbiased estimate of $\bar L(\theta)$ for any <em>fixed</em> $\theta$.`,
  R`<b>Random or deterministic?</b> $\bar L(\theta)$, $\theta^\ast$, $R$, $b$, $a$: deterministic. $L(\theta)$ (depends on the data), $\hat R$, $\hat b$, $\hat\theta$, $\hat X=Z\hat\theta$: random variables. A trap: $\bar L(\hat\theta)$ is random because $\hat\theta$ is, whereas $L(\theta^\ast)$ is random because $L$ is.`,
  R`<b>Key inequalities.</b> $L(\hat\theta)\le L(\theta^\ast)$ always (LS minimises the training loss), while $\bar L(\theta^\ast)\le\bar L(\hat\theta)$ (MMSE minimises expected loss). Training error is therefore optimistic; evaluate on held-out data. As $N\to\infty$, $\hat\theta\to\theta^\ast$ and both losses converge.`,
  R`<b>Advantages.</b> LS: needs only data, no model, adapts to the real (non-Gaussian) statistics of images; but it is noisy for small $N$, requires $N\gg p$ so $\hat R$ is well conditioned, and overfits. MMSE: optimal in expectation and free of sampling noise; but $R$ and $b$ are rarely known and must be modelled or, in practice, estimated, which brings you back to LS.`,
  R`<b>Computing $L(\theta^\ast)$ or $\bar L(\theta^\ast)$.</b> Plug in: $\bar L(\theta^\ast)=a-2b^tR^{-1}b+b^tR^{-1}RR^{-1}b=a-b^tR^{-1}b$. With numbers given for $a,b,R$ (or $\hat a,\hat b,\hat R$) carry the matrix algebra through; state units (squared gray levels).`,
  R`<b>Gaussian case.</b> If $(x_s,z_s)$ are jointly Gaussian, $E[x_s|z_s]$ is affine, so the MMSE estimator is the linear filter $\hat x_s=z_s\theta^\ast$ (plus a bias if means are non-zero) and no nonlinear estimator can do better. Images are not Gaussian, which is why CNNs and other nonlinear denoisers beat linear filters and why linear filters blur edges (2018 final).`,
  R`<b>Deblurring / restoration filter design.</b> Training pairs $(X,Y)$ = clean and corrupted images; $z_s$ = the $p$-pixel window of $Y$ around $s$; interior pixels only ($S_0$). $\hat\theta$ is the FIR filter's impulse response (space invariant because the same $\theta$ is used everywhere). Applying it is $\hat x_s=z_s\hat\theta$ for every pixel. Testing data must be disjoint from training data.`,
  R`<b>Orthogonality principle.</b> $E[(x_s-z_s\theta^\ast)z_s^t]=b-R\theta^\ast=0$: the MMSE error is uncorrelated with the data. Sample version: $Z^t(X-Z\hat\theta)=0$, the normal equations.`,
  R`<b>Quadratic norms and the HVS.</b> Minimising $E\|X-\hat X\|_B^2$ with $B=H^tH$ (a contrast-sensitivity weighting) has the same minimiser $E[X|Y]$ and the same optimal linear filter, so MMSE is not "wrong" for perception, just incomplete; choose the domain ($L^\ast$ or gamma-corrected) sensibly.`,
  R`<b>Bookkeeping.</b> $Z$ is $N\times p$, $\theta$ is $p\times1$, $\hat R$ is $p\times p$; the $1/N$ cancels in $\hat\theta$ but not in the losses; do not forget the constant term $a$ when comparing losses; write $R^{-1}$ only after asserting $R$ is positive definite.`,
  R`<b>Connections.</b> AR predictor fitting (final RP topic) and MMSE Exam 2 topic are the same problem with different $Z$; the projection-matrix view links to the pseudo-inverse and to regularisation (ridge: $(\hat R+\lambda I)^{-1}\hat b$) when $N$ is small.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/final/lti"] = [
  R`<b>Homogeneous, additive, linear.</b> $T$ is homogeneous if $T[\alpha x]=\alpha T[x]$ for all real $\alpha$; additive if $T[x+y]=T[x]+T[y]$; linear if both, i.e. $T[\alpha x+\beta y]=\alpha T[x]+\beta T[y]$. Homogeneity is much weaker than linearity (NonLinFilter notes).`,
  R`<b>Matrix maps are linear.</b> $f(x)=Ax$: $A(\alpha x)=\alpha Ax$ (homogeneous) and $A(x+y)=Ax+Ay$ (additive), both from the distributive property of matrix multiplication. Write the two lines out; that is the proof.`,
  R`<b>Every finite-dimensional linear map is a matrix.</b> Expand $x=\sum_ix_i\delta^{(i)}$ in the standard basis; linearity gives $T[x]=\sum_ix_iT[\delta^{(i)}]=Ax$ where column $i$ of $A$ is $T[\delta^{(i)}]$. The impulse responses are the columns.`,
  R`<b>Median is homogeneous but not linear.</b> Scaling by $\alpha>0$ preserves order and by $\alpha&lt;0$ reverses it, so $\mathrm{median}(\alpha x)=\alpha\,\mathrm{median}(x)$. Not additive: with a 3-point window, $\mathrm{median}[1,0,0]+\mathrm{median}[0,1,0]=0+0$ but $\mathrm{median}[1,1,0]=1$. Any specific counterexample earns full credit. The median also solves $\arg\min_\theta\sum_{r\in W}|\theta-x_{s+r}|$.`,
  R`<b>Linear systems on sequences.</b> The most general linear map on sequences is $y_n=\sum_mh_{n,m}x_m$ (an infinite matrix); $h_{n,m}$ is the response at $n$ to an impulse at $m$.`,
  R`<b>Time invariance.</b> With the shift $S_k[x]_n=x_{n-k}$, $T$ is time (shift) invariant iff $T\circ S_k=S_k\circ T$ for every $k$. This only makes sense for sequences on all of $\mathbb Z$ (or $\mathbb Z^2$), which is why finite-length images need boundary conventions.`,
  R`<b>LTI $\Rightarrow$ convolution.</b> Shift invariance forces $h_{n,m}=h_{n-m}$, so $y_n=\sum_mh_{n-m}x_m=(h*x)_n$; conversely convolution is linear and shift invariant. In 2-D: $y(m,n)=\sum_{k,l}h(k,l)x(m-k,n-l)$, $(2N+1)^2$ multiplies per pixel for an FIR of radius $N$ (Filters notes).`,
  R`<b>Eigenfunction property.</b> $x_n=e^{j\omega n}\Rightarrow y_n=\sum_kh_ke^{j\omega(n-k)}=H(e^{j\omega})e^{j\omega n}$ with $H(e^{j\omega})=\sum_kh_ke^{-j\omega k}$. A real cosine input gives $|H|\cos(\omega n+\angle H)$. Hence the frequency response characterises an LTI system completely.`,
  R`<b>Combining LTI systems.</b> Parallel sum: $h=a+b$, $H=A+B$. Cascade: $h=a*b$, $H=AB$ (order irrelevant). DC gain $=\sum_kh_k=H(e^{j0})$; image filters are normalised to DC gain 1 so mean brightness is preserved.`,
  R`<b>Non-examples to quote.</b> $y_n=x_n^2$: time invariant, not linear (fails homogeneity: $\alpha^2\ne\alpha$). $y_n=nx_n$: linear, not time invariant. Down-sampling $y_n=x_{2n}$: linear, not time invariant. Median: homogeneous, not additive, but shift invariant. Bilateral filter: shift invariant, not even homogeneous.`,
  R`<b>Separable 2-D FIR filters.</b> $h(m,n)=h_1(m)h_1(n)\Rightarrow H(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})=H_1(e^{j\mu})H_1(e^{j\nu})$. Smoothing $h_1=\tfrac14[1,2,1]$: $H_1=\tfrac12(1+\cos\omega)$, so $H=\tfrac14(1+\cos\mu)(1+\cos\nu)$, low-pass with $H(0,0)=1$ and zero at $(\pi,\cdot)$ or $(\cdot,\pi)$. Second difference $\tfrac14[1,-2,1]$: $H=\tfrac14(1-\cos\mu)(1-\cos\nu)$, high-pass with $H(0,0)=0$.`,
  R`<b>Sharpening (unsharp masking).</b> $g=\delta+\lambda(\delta-h)$ with $h$ a unit-DC-gain low-pass: $G=1+\lambda(1-H)$. DC gain $G(0,0)=1$; where $H\approx0$ the gain is $1+\lambda$. $\lambda>0$ boosts high frequencies (sharpens, may ring and amplify noise); $-1&lt;\lambda&lt;0$ attenuates them (blurs); $\lambda=-1$ gives $h$ itself. Sketch $G$ along $\mu$ for $\nu=0$ using $H_1$ above (2025 final).`,
  R`<b>Boundary conditions and space variance.</b> Zero padding (free boundary) breaks shift invariance near the border; periodic extension keeps circular shift invariance. State the assumption when computing outputs by hand.`,
  R`<b>Proof hygiene.</b> Quantify: "for all $\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb R$ and all inputs". To disprove, one explicit counterexample suffices; to prove, argue for arbitrary inputs. Distinguish linear (about superposition) from time invariant (about shifts); they are independent properties.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/final/rpfinal"] = [
  R`<b>AR model recap.</b> $y_n=x_n+\sum_{i=1}^Ph_iy_{n-i}$ with white $x_n$ ($\sigma^2$): $H=\dfrac1{1-\sum_ih_ie^{-j\omega i}}$, $S_y=\dfrac{\sigma^2}{|1-\sum_ih_ie^{-j\omega i}|^2}$; the causal MMSE predictor is $\hat y_n=E[y_n|y_{i&lt;n}]=\sum_ih_iy_{n-i}$ exactly, with prediction error $x_n$, because $x_n$ is independent of the past (FiltRandProc notes; Exam 2 RP topic).`,
  R`<b>Autocovariance of filtered white noise.</b> $R_y(k)=\sigma^2\sum_nh(n)h(n+k)$. Example $h=\delta-\tfrac12\delta_{n-1}-\tfrac12\delta_{n+1}$: $R_y(0)=\sigma^2(1+\tfrac14+\tfrac14)=\tfrac32\sigma^2$, $R_y(\pm1)=\sigma^2(-\tfrac12-\tfrac12)=-\sigma^2$, $R_y(\pm2)=\tfrac14\sigma^2$; $S_y=\sigma^2(1-\cos\omega)^2$. Check: $\tfrac1{2\pi}\int S_y=\tfrac32\sigma^2$.`,
  R`<b>Analysis flow (2024 final).</b> Given a texture $y$: (1) form regressors $z_n=[y_{n-1},\dots,y_{n-P}]$; (2) least-squares fit $\hat\theta=\hat R^{-1}\hat b$; (3) prediction residual $\epsilon_n=y_n-z_n\hat\theta$, approximately white with variance $\hat\sigma_\epsilon^2=\tfrac1N\sum\epsilon_n^2$; (4) normalise $w_n=\epsilon_n/\hat\sigma_\epsilon\sim N(0,1)$. Block diagram: $y\to$ FIR $1-\hat H$ $\to\epsilon\to\times1/\hat\sigma_\epsilon\to w$ (a whitening filter).`,
  R`<b>Synthesis flow.</b> Drive the inverse system with i.i.d. $N(0,1)$ noise: $w\to\times\hat\sigma_\epsilon\to\epsilon\to$ IIR $\dfrac1{1-\hat H}$ $\to y$, i.e. $y_n=\hat\sigma_\epsilon w_n+\sum_i\hat\theta_iy_{n-i}$ computed recursively in raster order from zero initial conditions. The synthesised texture has the same second-order statistics (PSD) as the original; for a Gaussian model that is everything.`,
  R`<b>2-D separable AR texture.</b> $Y(m,n)=X(m,n)+aY(m-1,n)+bY(m,n-1)-abY(m-1,n-1)$: $1-H=(1-ae^{-j\mu})(1-be^{-j\nu})$, so $H_{\rm sys}=\dfrac1{(1-ae^{-j\mu})(1-be^{-j\nu})}$, impulse response $a^mb^nu(m)u(n)$ (product of two first-order 1-D systems), $$S_Y(e^{j\mu},e^{j\nu})=\frac{\sigma^2}{|1-ae^{-j\mu}|^2\,|1-be^{-j\nu}|^2},$$ $R_Y(k,l)=\dfrac{\sigma^2a^{|k|}b^{|l|}}{(1-a^2)(1-b^2)}$. MMSE causal predictor: the recursion without $X$. Spectrum peaks at DC and is more concentrated along the axis with the larger coefficient (stronger correlation along that direction).`,
  R`<b>Stability.</b> 1-D: poles inside the unit circle; a least-squares predictor fitted from data always yields a stable synthesis filter. 2-D: not guaranteed; also the causal ordering (raster) can cause asymmetric artifacts and IIR cost grows as $2(P+1)P$ multiplies per pixel (Filters notes).`,
  R`<b>Causal orderings in the plane.</b> Quarter plane ($s_1&lt;r_1$ and $s_2&lt;r_2$), symmetric half plane ($s_2&lt;r_2$), non-symmetric half plane ($s_2&lt;r_2$ or ($s_2=r_2$ and $s_1&lt;r_1$)); recursive filters must use a past defined by one of these. Example: $y(m,n)=x+ay(m-1,n)+ay(m,n-1)$ gives $H=\dfrac1{1-ae^{-j\mu}-ae^{-j\nu}}$; adding $2ay(m+1,n-1)$ (NSHP) tilts the response diagonally; adding $y(m+1,n)$ and $y(m,n+1)$ is non-causal and cannot be computed recursively ("this doesn't work").`,
  R`<b>Filtering WSS processes.</b> $S_y=|H|^2S_x$ and $R_y=h*\tilde h*R_x$; for separable $h$ the PSD factorises; output variance $=\tfrac1{(2\pi)^2}\iint S_y$. For a first-order 1-D system $|H|^2=1/(1+a^2-2a\cos\omega)$ with extremes $1/(1\mp a)^2$.`,
  R`<b>Spectral estimation.</b> Periodogram averaging over $K$ blocks of $N$ samples: variance falls with $K$, resolution improves with $N$; the AR estimate $\hat\sigma^2/|1-\hat H|^2$ is smooth and parametric. The expectation in the PSD definition is essential (RandProc notes).`,
  R`<b>Prediction error properties.</b> Errors are uncorrelated with each other and with the past (iterated expectation); the prediction variance $\sigma^2=E[X_s^2]$ is the innovation power; for Gaussian processes the optimal predictor is linear and space invariant, $\hat Y_s=\sum_{r>0}h_rY_{s-r}$.`,
  R`<b>White noise facts.</b> i.i.d. $N(0,\sigma^2)$: $R=\sigma^2\delta$, $S=\sigma^2$, strict-sense stationary; any deterministic pattern of repetition or modulation breaks stationarity; the sum of independent WSS processes has $S=S_1+S_2$.`,
  R`<b>Sketching $S_y$ in 2-D.</b> Evaluate at $(0,0)$, $(\pi,0)$, $(0,\pi)$, $(\pi,\pi)$: e.g. separable AR with $a=b=0.9$ gives $\sigma^2/0.01^2$ at DC and $\sigma^2/1.9^4$ at the corner; draw contours elongated along the more correlated axis; note $2\pi$ periodicity in both variables.`,
  R`<b>Realisations and randomness.</b> The texture $Y$, the periodogram, $\hat\theta$ and $\hat\sigma^2$ are random; $S_Y$, $R_Y$, $\theta$ are deterministic. Two synthesised textures from different seeds differ pixel by pixel but share the spectrum.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/final/mri"] = [
  R`<b>Larmor precession.</b> Nuclear spins in a field of magnitude $M$ precess at $\omega_0=\gamma M$ (rad/s; the notes write the constant as $L$). A gradient makes the field position dependent: $M(x,t)=M_0+xG(t)$, so $\omega(x,t)=\omega_0+x\gamma G(t)$ (MRI notes; Exam 1 MRI topic).`,
  R`<b>Phase accumulation.</b> Frequency is the time derivative of phase, so $\phi(x,t)=\int_0^t\omega(x,\tau)d\tau=\omega_0t+x\,k(t)$ with $$k(t)=\gamma\int_0^tG(\tau)\,d\tau$$ (rad per unit length). The gradient waveform's integral, not its value, sets the spatial frequency.`,
  R`<b>Received signal.</b> Each element emits $r(x,t)=a(x)e^{j\phi(x,t)}$ with $a(x)$ the tissue-dependent weighting (proton density, $T_1$, $T_2$). Summing over the rod: $r(t)=\int a(x)e^{j(\omega_0t+xk(t))}dx=e^{j\omega_0t}\int a(x)e^{jxk(t)}dx=e^{j\omega_0t}A\big(-\tfrac{k(t)}{2\pi}\big)$, where $A(f)=\int a(x)e^{-j2\pi fx}dx$ is the CTFT in cycles per unit length. Mind the sign: the sample is at $f=-k(t)/2\pi$.`,
  R`<b>k-space.</b> After demodulation $s(t)=r(t)e^{-j\omega_0t}$ the receiver reads the Fourier transform of the object at the spatial frequency $-k(t)/2\pi$; the gradient waveform steers a trajectory through k-space. In 2-D, $k_x(t)=\gamma\int G_x$, $k_y(t)=\gamma\int G_y$ and $s(t)=F(-k_x/2\pi,-k_y/2\pi)$.`,
  R`<b>Constant readout gradient.</b> $G(t)=G_0$ for $t\ge0$ gives $k(t)=\gamma G_0t$: a straight line in k-space starting at $k=0$; a negative pre-phasing lobe of area $-\gamma G_0T_p$ first moves to $k_{\min}$ so the readout sweeps symmetrically through $k=0$ (the echo).`,
  R`<b>Sampling in k-space.</b> Sampling $s(t)$ every $\Delta t$ samples $A$ at spacing $\Delta f=\gamma G_0\Delta t/2\pi$; by the (dual) sampling theorem the reconstructed object is periodic with field of view $\mathrm{FOV}=1/\Delta f$, so $\Delta f\le1/(\text{object width})$ avoids wrap-around aliasing. The largest $|k|$ reached sets the resolution: $\Delta x\approx1/(2f_{\max})$.`,
  R`<b>Reconstruction recipe.</b> (1) demodulate by $e^{-j\omega_0t}$; (2) assign sample $n$ to $A(f_n)$ with $f_n=-k(t_n)/2\pi$ (reverse order because of the minus sign); (3) arrange so $f=0$ is at the centre; (4) inverse DFT with the correct scaling $\Delta f$. Result: $a(x)$ sampled at $\Delta x=\mathrm{FOV}/N$.`,
  R`<b>Slice selection.</b> Turn on $G_z$ only so $\omega=\gamma(M_0+zG_z)$; an RF pulse $e^{j2\pi f_ct}\mathrm{sinc}(t\Delta f)$ has spectrum $\mathrm{rect}((f-f_c)/\Delta f)$ and excites only spins whose Larmor frequency lies in that band: slice centre $z_c=(2\pi f_c/\gamma-M_0)/G_z$, thickness $2\pi\Delta f/(\gamma G_z)$.`,
  R`<b>2-D Cartesian acquisition.</b> For each line: a $G_y$ blip whose area sets $k_y$ (phase encoding), a negative $G_x$ lobe, then constant $G_x$ readout sampling $k_x$ across the line. Repeat for all $k_y$; 2-D inverse FFT. Echo-planar imaging traverses a serpentine path in one shot.`,
  R`<b>Sampling theory in reverse.</b> MRI samples the frequency domain: $\Delta k$ plays the role of $1/\mathrm{FOV}$ (replication in space), $k_{\max}$ the role of the band limit (resolution), and the reconstruction is a DFT rather than sinc interpolation. Compare with CT, which samples projections (Radon domain) and needs the Fourier slice theorem.`,
  R`<b>Units check.</b> $\gamma$ in rad/(s$\cdot$T), $G$ in T/m, $k$ in rad/m, $f=k/2\pi$ in cycles/m, $A(f)$ in the units of $a$ times length. $\omega_0t$ is the carrier phase and cancels on demodulation.`,
  R`<b>Typical exam asks.</b> Write $\omega(x,t)$, integrate to $\phi$, define $k(t)$; show $r(t)=e^{j\omega_0t}A(-k/2\pi)$; sketch $k(t)$ for a given $G(t)$ (piecewise linear, slope $\gamma G$); describe reconstruction; state how sampling interval and readout duration set FOV and resolution.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/final/halftone"] = [
  R`<b>Halftoning.</b> Rendering continuous tone with a binary device (ink or no ink) by exploiting the eye's low-pass response. Work in absorptance units for printing (larger = darker; 255 = black) and lightness for display; a space-varying threshold $T(i,j)$ gives $b(i,j)=255$ if $X(i,j)>T(i,j)$, else 0 (Halftoning notes).`,
  R`<b>Fixed threshold.</b> $T=127$ is the minimum-squared-error one-bit quantizer but destroys all tone: large flat black and white regions. Good halftones accept larger pixel-wise error to obtain the right <em>local average</em>.`,
  R`<b>Ordered dither (screens).</b> An $N\times N$ index matrix $I(i,j)$ lists the order in which pixels turn on; the threshold matrix $T(i,j)=255\,\dfrac{I(i,j)+0.5}{N^2}$ is tiled periodically, $b=255$ if $X>T(i\bmod N,j\bmod N)$. A screen renders $N^2+1$ gray levels; e.g. $8\times8$ gives 65 levels, $16\times16$ all 257 but at half the spatial resolution.`,
  R`<b>Clustered vs. dispersed dot.</b> Clustered-dot screens turn on neighbouring pixels consecutively: visible texture, poor detail, but robust to dot gain, spot variability and noise (laser printers). Dispersed-dot (Bayer, $I_{2n}=\begin{bmatrix}4I_n+1&4I_n+2\\4I_n+3&4I_n\end{bmatrix}$) spreads thresholds uniformly: better detail, no resolution/levels trade-off, but needs stable isolated dots; void-and-cluster screens improve on Bayer.`,
  R`<b>White-noise (random threshold) screen analysis.</b> Constant gray $g\in[0,1]$, thresholds $T_{i,j}$ i.i.d. $U[0,1]$, $b=1$ if $g\ge T$: $P\{b=1\}=g$, $E[b]=g$, $\mathrm{Var}(b)=g(1-g)$. Display error $d=b-g$ has mean 0 and autocovariance $g(1-g)\delta(k,l)$ (independent thresholds), so its PSD is flat, $S_d=g(1-g)$: white noise. The process is strict-sense stationary because the joint law does not depend on position.`,
  R`<b>Why white-noise screens look bad.</b> White error has as much power at low frequencies as high; the luminance CSF passes low and mid frequencies, so the noise is visible as grain. Good halftones (error diffusion, blue-noise masks) shape the error spectrum to be high-pass (blue), where the eye's MTF attenuates it. Error power alone is not the metric; HVS-weighted error is.`,
  R`<b>Error diffusion equations.</b> Raster scan; $\tilde f(i,j)=f(i,j)+\sum_{k,l\in S}h(k,l)e(i-k,j-l)$, $b=255$ if $\tilde f>T$ else 0, $e=\tilde f-b$; $h\ge0$ sums to 1 over the causal neighbourhood. Floyd–Steinberg weights $\tfrac1{16}[7;\ 3,5,1]$, Jarvis–Judice–Ninke a $5\times3$ set over 48. "Pulling errors forward" (IIR view) and "pushing errors ahead" are equivalent.`,
  R`<b>Spectral analysis of error diffusion.</b> Rearranging the block diagram: $b=f-(\delta-h)*e$, so the display error is $f-b=(\delta-h)*e$, a high-pass filtered ($1-H$, zero at DC) version of the quantization error. In 1-D with $h=\delta_{n-1}$ (sigma–delta modulation): $\tilde f_n=f_n+e_{n-1}$, $b_n=f_n+e_{n-1}-e_n$, $B(e^{j\omega})=F-(1-e^{-j\omega})E$, $|1-e^{-j\omega}|=2|\sin(\omega/2)|$. The 2016 class-D amplifier is exactly this loop; an RC or the eye low-passes the result back to $f$.`,
  R`<b>Quantization error model.</b> $e$ assumed uniform on $[-\tfrac12,\tfrac12]$ (in unit steps), uncorrelated in space, independent of $\tilde f$, variance $1/12$: $E[e(i,j)e(i+k,j+l)]=\tfrac1{12}\delta(k,l)$. Then $S_{f-b}=|1-H|^2/12$: a designed noise spectrum.`,
  R`<b>Unsharp masking side effect.</b> In reality $e$ correlates with the image: $E=\rho F+R$ ($\rho\approx0.55$ Floyd–Steinberg, $0.8$ JJN, 0 in 1-D), giving $B=[1-\rho(1-H)]F+$ noise, a sharpening of the image. Explains the crisp look of error-diffused images.`,
  R`<b>Design objectives.</b> Match local mean (DC gain of the loop is 1); push error to high frequencies; avoid periodic worms and limit cycles (serpentine scan, threshold modulation, tone-dependent ED); measure quality with the radially averaged power spectrum and HVS-weighted least squares.`,
  R`<b>Colour halftoning link.</b> Use linear (absorptance or luminance) units for averaging, and evaluate error in an opponent space filtered by luminance and chrominance CSFs (YCxCz/Lab metric) because chrominance errors are tolerated at lower frequencies (ColorFidelityMetrics notes).`,
  R`<b>Exam mechanics.</b> Compute $E[b]$, $\mathrm{Var}(b)$, autocovariance and PSD with the indicator trick $b=\mathbf 1\{g\ge T\}$; state stationarity with a reason; write the ED difference equations and take the DTFT/DSFT to show the high-pass shaping; explain visibility with the CSF.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/final/bilateral"] = [
  R`<b>Definition.</b> $y_s=\sum_{r\in W}w_{s,r}x_r$ with $w_{s,r}=\tilde w_{s,r}/\sum_k\tilde w_{s,k}$ and $$\tilde w_{s,r}=\exp\Big\{-\frac{\|s-r\|^2}{2\sigma_s^2}\Big\}\exp\Big\{-\frac{(x_s-x_r)^2}{2\sigma_x^2}\Big\}:$$ a Gaussian spatial blur whose weights are also suppressed for pixels whose values differ greatly from the centre pixel (the range kernel).`,
  R`<b>Purpose.</b> Edge-preserving denoising: average pixels on the same side of an edge, exclude those across it. Linear filters cannot do this because they blur edges (they are optimal only for Gaussian images); the bilateral filter is a simple nonlinear alternative (NonLinFilter, OptLinFilter notes).`,
  R`<b>Why normalise.</b> Dividing by $\sum_k\tilde w_{s,k}$ makes the weights sum to 1, so a constant image is reproduced exactly (unit DC gain) and the local brightness is unchanged even when many neighbours are excluded near an edge or in a textured region.`,
  R`<b>Not linear (proof).</b> Homogeneity fails: for a tiny impulse $\epsilon\delta$ the range kernel is $\approx1$ everywhere and the output is the Gaussian-blurred impulse $\epsilon g$; for a huge impulse $A\delta$ the centre pixel's neighbours all have very different values, weights collapse onto the centre and the output is $\approx A\delta$, not $Ag$. So $T[Ax]\ne AT[x]$; a fortiori not additive.`,
  R`<b>Space invariant.</b> The weights depend only on the displacement $s-r$ and on the pixel values $x_s,x_r$, never on absolute position, so shifting the input shifts the output: $T\circ S_k=S_k\circ T$ (up to boundaries). Nonlinear systems can still be shift invariant.`,
  R`<b>Choosing $\sigma_x$.</b> Set it comparable to (one to three times) the noise standard deviation and much smaller than the edge contrast: same-region pixels differ by noise and stay in the average, cross-edge pixels differ by the step and are excluded. Limits: $\sigma_x\to\infty$ gives plain Gaussian smoothing; $\sigma_x\to0$ gives the identity (only exactly equal values count).`,
  R`<b>Choosing $\sigma_s$.</b> Sets the spatial extent (window radius about $3\sigma_s$). Larger $\sigma_s$ averages more pixels and removes more noise in flat regions but costs more; because the range kernel already protects edges, $\sigma_s$ can be larger than for a linear Gaussian without smearing them. Very large $\sigma_s$ with small $\sigma_x$ tends to flatten regions (cartoon look).`,
  R`<b>Behaviour at a step edge.</b> If the step $\Delta\gg\sigma_x$, the output at a pixel is approximately the mean of the same-side pixels: the edge stays sharp and noise on both sides is reduced. If $\Delta\lesssim\sigma_x$ the edge is blurred like a Gaussian. Impulsive noise: an outlier centre pixel has no similar neighbours and is <em>kept</em>; the median is better for salt-and-pepper.`,
  R`<b>Relation to the median.</b> The median is homogeneous but not additive and solves $\arg\min_\theta\sum_r|\theta-x_{s+r}|$; weighted medians use weights $a_r$. The bilateral filter is not even homogeneous, but like the median it is a robust, edge-preserving, shift-invariant neighbourhood operation.`,
  R`<b>Cost and limitations.</b> $O(|W|)$ exponentials per pixel and no frequency-domain analysis or FFT implementation; iterating the filter flattens textures; gradient reversal/halo artifacts near strong edges; parameters must track the noise level (estimate $\sigma$ from a flat patch first).`,
  R`<b>Data-driven view.</b> Both the bilateral weights and the MMSE linear filter estimate $E[X_s|Y_{W(s)}]$; the bilateral filter is a hand-designed nonlinear $f(Y_{W(s)})$ appropriate because images are not Gaussian. Modern denoisers (non-local means, CNNs) generalise the same idea.`,
  R`<b>Exam mechanics.</b> Write the two Gaussian factors and the normalisation; demonstrate non-linearity with the scaled impulse; argue space invariance from the dependence on $s-r$ only; relate $\sigma_x$ to noise $\sigma$ and edge height $\Delta$ with an inequality $\sigma\lesssim\sigma_x\ll\Delta$.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/final/colorfinal"] = [
  R`<b>The two matrices.</b> If $[r,g,b]^t=B[X,Y,Z]^t$ (display rgb from XYZ), then $[X,Y,Z]^t=B^{-1}[r,g,b]^t=M[r,g,b]^t$ with $M=B^{-1}$. Columns of $M$ are the XYZ tristimulus values of the R, G, B primaries ($r=1$, others 0); rows of $B$ applied to the XYZ CMFs give the display's rgb CMFs: $[r_0,g_0,b_0]^t(\lambda)=B[x_0,y_0,z_0]^t(\lambda)$ (Tristimulus, ColorSpaces notes).`,
  R`<b>White point from $B$.</b> Display white is $(r,g,b)=(1,1,1)$, so $[X_w,Y_w,Z_w]^t=B^{-1}\mathbf 1=M\mathbf 1$ (sum of the columns of $M$); its chromaticity is $x_w=X_w/(X_w+Y_w+Z_w)$, $y_w=Y_w/(X_w+Y_w+Z_w)$. Compare with D65 $(0.3127,0.3290)$ or EE $(\tfrac13,\tfrac13)$ to identify the standard.`,
  R`<b>Primary chromaticities from $B$.</b> The red primary's XYZ is the first column of $B^{-1}$; normalise to get $(x_r,y_r)$; likewise green and blue from columns 2 and 3. Plot them on the diagram to draw the gamut triangle. If $M$ is given instead, use its columns directly.`,
  R`<b>rgb CMFs cannot all be positive.</b> Real primaries lie inside the horseshoe, so the spectral colours outside their triangle require negative tristimulus values; equivalently the rows of $B$ mix the non-negative XYZ CMFs with negative coefficients (e.g. sRGB: $r_0=3.24x_0-1.54y_0-0.50z_0$), producing negative lobes. Only imaginary primaries (like XYZ) yield non-negative CMFs.`,
  R`<b>Chromaticity essentials.</b> $x=\dfrac X{X+Y+Z}$, $y=\dfrac Y{X+Y+Z}$, $z=1-x-y$; scale invariant; straight lines map to straight lines, so mixtures lie on segments and three primaries span a triangle. Spectral locus, line of purples, real colours inside; XYZ primaries at $(1,0),(0,1),(0,0)$.`,
  R`<b>Real vs. displayable vs. positive XYZ.</b> Real: inside the horseshoe. Displayable: inside the primaries' triangle, i.e. $(r,g,b)=B[x,y,1-x-y]^t$ all $\ge0$. Positive XYZ: inside the big XYZ triangle, which includes non-real points. Shade the region $r&lt;0$ as the part of the horseshoe beyond the G–B edge.`,
  R`<b>Building $M$ from chromaticities and a white point.</b> $M=A\,\mathrm{diag}(\alpha)$ with columns of $A$ the primary chromaticities $[x_k,y_k,z_k]^t$; solve $A\alpha=\tfrac1{y_w}[x_w,y_w,z_w]^t$ so that $(1,1,1)$ gives the white point with $Y=1$. For 709/D65 this is the sRGB matrix $\begin{bmatrix}0.4124&0.3576&0.1805\\0.2126&0.7152&0.0722\\0.0193&0.1192&0.9505\end{bmatrix}$; then $B=M^{-1}$ (Exam 2 colorimetry topic).`,
  R`<b>Gamma correction in the pipeline.</b> $B$ acts on linear values. From XYZ: $[r,g,b]_{\rm lin}=B[X,Y,Z]^t$, clip to $[0,1]$, then $\tilde r=255\,r_{\rm lin}^{1/\gamma}$ ($\gamma\approx2.2$; exact sRGB curve with linear toe). From an 8-bit image: $r_{\rm lin}=(\tilde r/255)^\gamma$ in floating point before applying $M$. Never apply $B$ or $M$ to gamma-corrected values.`,
  R`<b>Luminance row.</b> The middle row of $M$ gives $Y=\sum_kM_{2k}r_k$; with the white point normalised to $Y=1$ these coefficients sum to 1 (0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722 for sRGB). Video luma $\tilde Y$ uses similar weights on gamma-corrected values by convention.`,
  R`<b>Camera side.</b> Camera sensitivities are non-negative, so they can approximate XYZ (or any positive combination) but not the display's rgb CMFs directly; a $3\times3$ matrix maps camera values toward XYZ and then $B$ maps to display rgb; colours outside the display gamut are clipped or gamut-mapped.`,
  R`<b>Metamers and Grassmann.</b> Two spectra with equal XYZ look identical under the same conditions; tristimulus values are linear in the spectrum ($[X,Y,Z]^t=M_{xyz}I$ with CMF rows), which is what makes all the matrix algebra above valid.`,
  R`<b>Sanity checks.</b> $M\mathbf 1$ should be near the intended white; chromaticities of the columns should look like red, green, blue; $\det M>0$; the rgb CMFs integrate to roughly 1 each for an EE-normalised system.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/final/cnn"] = [
  R`<b>Convolution layer as a filter bank.</b> A layer with $C_{\rm in}$ input channels, $C_{\rm out}$ output channels and $k\times k$ kernels computes, for each output channel, a sum over input channels of 2-D convolutions plus a bias: an LSI (space-invariant) linear map followed by a pointwise nonlinearity (ReLU). It is the learned analogue of the LS/MMSE FIR filter with $\theta$ trained by gradient descent (OptLinFilter notes).`,
  R`<b>Parameter count.</b> Weights $k^2C_{\rm in}C_{\rm out}$ plus $C_{\rm out}$ biases: $k^2C_{\rm in}C_{\rm out}+C_{\rm out}$. Example: $3\times3$, 64 in, 128 out: $9\cdot64\cdot128+128=73{,}856$. The weight tensor has shape $C_{\rm out}\times C_{\rm in}\times k\times k$ (rank 4) and the bias shape $C_{\rm out}$ (rank 1). Sum over layers for the total.`,
  R`<b>Output size and cost.</b> With input $N\times N$, padding $P$, stride $S$: output $\lfloor(N+2P-k)/S\rfloor+1$ per side; multiply-adds per layer $\approx N_{\rm out}^2k^2C_{\rm in}C_{\rm out}$. Compare a fully connected layer on an $N^2C$ input: $N^2C\times$ outputs parameters, far more.`,
  R`<b>Why convolutional.</b> Weight sharing gives translation equivariance (features detected anywhere), far fewer parameters, less data and compute, and a receptive field that grows with depth (each $3\times3$ layer adds 2 pixels; pooling or stride multiplies it).`,
  R`<b>Training, validation, test sets.</b> Train: fit the parameters. Validation: choose hyper-parameters (architecture, learning rate, stopping epoch). Test: touched once, at the end, to report performance. Report the <em>test</em> loss in a paper; never tune on it. Performance on training data is always better than on test data (same lesson as LS filters).`,
  R`<b>Why test loss exceeds training loss.</b> The parameters were chosen to minimise the training loss, so the training loss is an optimistic (biased) estimate; the gap is overfitting, worst when the model has many parameters relative to the data. Statistically: $L(\hat\theta)\le L(\theta^\ast)$ on the training set while $\bar L(\theta^\ast)\le\bar L(\hat\theta)$ in expectation.`,
  R`<b>Reducing the gap.</b> More (or augmented) training data; regularisation (weight decay, dropout, early stopping chosen on the validation set); a smaller or simpler model; batch normalisation; transfer learning. If both losses are high the model is under-fitting: make it bigger or train longer.`,
  R`<b>Loss functions.</b> Regression (denoising, restoration): MSE, which for Gaussian noise is the negative log-likelihood; classification: cross-entropy on softmax outputs. MSE is minimised by $E[X|Y]$, so a well-trained CNN approximates the conditional mean, a nonlinear MMSE estimator (why CNNs beat Wiener filters on non-Gaussian images).`,
  R`<b>Pooling and stride.</b> Max/average pooling or strided convolution reduce resolution (down-sampling; aliasing is accepted); transposed convolution or interpolation up-sample. Pooling has no parameters.`,
  R`<b>Optimisation basics.</b> Stochastic gradient descent on mini-batches with back-propagation (chain rule); learning rate and epochs are hyper-parameters; monitor training and validation curves and stop when validation loss starts rising.`,
  R`<b>Counting checklist.</b> Include biases; use input channels of <em>this</em> layer (the previous layer's output channels); $1\times1$ convolutions have $C_{\rm in}C_{\rm out}+C_{\rm out}$; fully connected layers have $n_{\rm in}n_{\rm out}+n_{\rm out}$; pooling, ReLU and flattening add none.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece637/final/misc"] = [
  R`<b>Rate–distortion for a Gaussian scalar.</b> $X\sim N(0,\sigma^2)$ with squared-error distortion: $R(\delta)=\max\{\tfrac12\log_2(\sigma^2/\delta),0\}$ bits, $D(\delta)=\min\{\sigma^2,\delta\}$, parametrised by $\delta$ (roughly the squared quantiser step). Rate is zero once $\delta\ge\sigma^2$ (send nothing, decode the mean); $R(D)$ is decreasing and convex (SourceCoding notes).`,
  R`<b>Independent Gaussian components.</b> For $X_n\sim N(0,\sigma_n^2)$ independent, $R(\delta)=\sum_n\max\{\tfrac12\log_2(\sigma_n^2/\delta),0\}$, $D(\delta)=\sum_n\min\{\sigma_n^2,\delta\}$: use the <em>same</em> $\delta$ for every component (reverse water-filling); components with variance below $\delta$ get zero bits. Rates and distortions add.`,
  R`<b>Correlated Gaussian vector.</b> $X\sim N(0,R)$, $R=E\Lambda E^t$: decorrelate with the KLT $\tilde X=E^tX$ (covariance $\Lambda$, independent components), code the components as above, invert with $E$. Squared error is preserved by the orthonormal transform, so the same $R(D)$ applies with $\sigma_n^2=\lambda_n$; this is why transform coding decorrelates first (eigen topic).`,
  R`<b>Information-theoretic backdrop.</b> Differential entropy $h(X)=-E[\log_2p(X)]$; mutual information $I(X;Z)=h(X)-h(X|Z)$; $R(D)=\inf\{I(X_0;Z):E\|X_0-Z\|^2\le D\}$; Shannon's source coding theorem says rates arbitrarily close to $R(D)$ are achievable with long block codes, and any quadratic norm $\|\cdot\|_B$ reduces to the plain case via $\tilde X=AX$.`,
  R`<b>Plane waves.</b> $E(\mathbf r,t)=E_0e^{j2\pi\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf r}e^{-j2\pi f_0t}$ satisfies the wave equation $\nabla^2E-\tfrac1{c^2}\partial_t^2E=0$ iff $-4\pi^2\|\mathbf v\|^2+4\pi^2f_0^2/c^2=0$, i.e. $c=f_0/\|\mathbf v\|$. Wavelength $\lambda=1/\|\mathbf v\|=c/f_0$ (3 cm at 10 GHz; 500 nm for green light at $6\times10^{14}$ Hz); light covers about 30 cm per nanosecond. The physical field is the real part $A_0\cos(2\pi(\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf r-f_0t)+\theta)$; wavefronts are planes perpendicular to $\mathbf v$ (FO notes).`,
  R`<b>Spatial-frequency view of a wave.</b> $[v_x,v_y,v_z]$ in cycles per unit length with $\|\mathbf v\|=1/\lambda$; propagating from $z=0$ to $z=d$ multiplies each 2-D frequency $(v_x,v_y)$ by $H=e^{j2\pi v_zd}$, $v_z=\sqrt{1/\lambda^2-v_x^2-v_y^2}$; the Fresnel approximation $H\approx e^{j2\pi d/\lambda}e^{-j\pi\lambda d(v_x^2+v_y^2)}$ is a quadratic-phase LSI system, and in the far field (Fraunhofer) the observed pattern is the Fourier transform of the aperture evaluated at $(x/\lambda d,y/\lambda d)$.`,
  R`<b>Ideal DT low-pass and windows.</b> $h(n)=T\,\mathrm{sinc}(Tn)$ with $T=\omega_c/\pi$ (so $H=\mathrm{prect}_{2\omega_c}$, gain 1); infinite and non-causal, so for streaming use truncate to $2N+1$ taps and delay. The rectangular window has the largest sidelobes ($-13$ dB, Gibbs ripple independent of $N$); Hamming/Hann/Kaiser trade a wider transition for lower ripple (Exam 2 LPF topic).`,
  R`<b>Windowing in frequency.</b> Multiplying $h$ by $w$ convolves $H$ with $W(e^{j\omega})$ (periodic convolution): main-lobe width sets the transition band ($\approx4\pi/(2N+1)$ for the rectangular window), sidelobe level sets pass- and stop-band ripple.`,
  R`<b>Entropy coding reminders.</b> Entropy $H=-\sum p_i\log_2p_i$ lower-bounds the average bits per symbol; Huffman achieves $H\le\bar L&lt;H+1$, block coding and arithmetic coding approach $H$; lossless methods give only modest compression (2:1), which is why lossy transform coding is used.`,
  R`<b>Incoherent vs. coherent light.</b> Independent random-phase fields add in power, fixed-phase fields add in amplitude and interfere (Exam 2 HVS topic); Fourier optics assumes a coherent monochromatic field.`,
  R`<b>Units in the odd problems.</b> $R$ in bits per sample, $D$ in squared units of $X$; $\mathbf v$ in cycles/m, $f_0$ in Hz, $c\approx3\times10^8$ m/s; $\omega_c$ in rad/sample and $T=\omega_c/\pi$ dimensionless.`,
  R`<b>Strategy.</b> These topics appear as one short problem: state the formula, verify it with a one-line derivation (substitute the plane wave; take the log of the Gaussian variance ratio; integrate the rect), and finish with the numerical value and its units.`
];
})();
